window.LESSONS = [{"file":"week-01-banking-chatbot-critique","weekLabel":"Week 1 · Method","title":"Repair a banking chatbot design","minutes":"90 min","company":"JPMorgan Chase / Revolut","timed":false,"brief":"A teammate draws one box labeled LLM that answers balances and sends money. You are in a design review in an hour. Repair the design before anyone builds it. This is the second session of week 1: the same four moves as the card tap, on a model instead of a terminal.","goal":"Separate language from money. The model may explain a balance it was just given. It may not be the place the balance lives, and it may not be the thing that moves the money.","idea":"A model has a deadline, a cost, and a habit of sounding sure when it is wrong. Money movement stays on an ordinary API. The model may call that API to read. The model is not the ledger.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapter 2 — Data models","ddiaBody":"Chapter 2 asks which store owns the facts. The chat is a log of what was said. The account balance is a different fact. If you mix them, the transcript starts to look like the balance, and a retry can invent a transfer.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour","barBody":"JPMorgan Chase asks who may see which account, and which tool call is written down for an auditor. Revolut asks whether a retry can move the same money twice. Google or Microsoft still want a deadline for the balance read, separate from the deadline for a payment.","caseBody":"Treat the Netflix personalization architecture as a warning, not a template to copy. They split offline generation from the online request because the online path has a deadline. A banking assistant has a stricter version of that split: the online path may call a read API, and a payment is a separate audited command.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/system-architectures-for-personalization-and-recommendation-e081aa94b5d8","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — System architectures for personalization","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"2M","label":"Monthly active app users"},{"value":"30 /s","label":"Peak chat turns"},{"value":"800 ms","label":"p99 for a balance answer"},{"value":"0","label":"Silent transfers allowed"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Balance read","Must come from the ledger at request time","Source of truth"],["Tool timeout","400 ms, then a typed failure","What the model may say"],["Audit retention","7 years for money-moving tool calls","What you store besides chat"],["Entitlement","The caller can read only their accounts","What retrieval must filter"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"tools","prompt":"What may the model do?","sound":"read-only","staff":"The stronger cut is read-only tools plus a separate, confirmed payment API the model cannot call by itself. Revolut and JPMorgan Chase will reject a design where the model is the system of record for a transfer.","options":[{"value":"read-only","label":"Read balances and transactions. Payments go through a confirmed API."},{"value":"full-tools","label":"The model can call transfer, with the prompt telling it to be careful."},{"value":"no-tools","label":"The model answers from memory of the user's last session."}]},{"id":"balance","prompt":"Where does a spoken balance come from?","sound":"ledger-read","staff":"Read the ledger, or a store you can prove is derived from it, on that request. Session memory and the model's parameters are not a balance. Chapter 2: pick the model that owns the fact.","options":[{"value":"ledger-read","label":"A ledger read on this request, scoped to entitled accounts."},{"value":"memory","label":"Whatever balance the model remembers from earlier in the chat."},{"value":"cache-day","label":"A balance cached this morning for the whole user base."}]},{"id":"audit","prompt":"What is the audit record?","sound":"tool-log","staff":"Every tool call is a row: user, entitlement, idempotency key, request, response, time. The chat transcript is not enough for a regulator, because it does not prove which API ran.","options":[{"value":"tool-log","label":"Durable tool-call log plus the chat transcript."},{"value":"chat-only","label":"The chat transcript is the audit."},{"value":"none","label":"Debug logs for two weeks, then delete."}]}],"failureTitle":"The model says the transfer succeeded. The payments API timed out.","failureBody":"The user asked to move 200 dollars. The tool call's deadline fired. You do not know whether the payments service committed. The model drafts 'Done, I sent 200 dollars.' Write what the user is allowed to see, and how a retry is identified.","reviewIntro":"A hire-level repair separates language from money. The model can explain a result it was given. It cannot invent a result.","review":["Sync path: check entitlement, read the ledger, and if the user is confirming a payment, call the payments API with an idempotency key created when they confirmed, not when the model felt ready. Async path: notifications and the audit archive.","On timeout, the assistant says the outcome is unknown and shows the tool-call id. It does not say the money moved. A retry uses the same key. JPMorgan Chase will ask for that sentence in the audit trail."],"rubric":[["Scope","Assistant reads. Payments are a different API."],["Estimates","p99 for a read, and a separate budget for a payment"],["Source of truth","Ledger for balances, tool log for what ran"],["Diagram","Model calls tools. Tools do not call the model for truth."],["Deep dive","The timeout copy the user sees"],["Failure","Unknown is a valid answer. Same key on retry."],["Tradeoff","A slower honest answer over a fast invented one"]]},{"file":"week-01-card-authorization","weekLabel":"Week 1 · Method","title":"Card authorization estimates","minutes":"90 min","company":"Visa / Capital One","timed":false,"brief":"A customer taps a card. Design only the yes, no, or 'we do not know yet' that the shop terminal needs while the customer is still standing there. Paying the shop happens later. You will name that later work and leave it out of this hour.","goal":"Practice the opening of a design: cut the scope, choose which number sizes disk and which number sizes speed, and say what a timeout must not do.","idea":"A good record of this tap is durable and unambiguous: approved, declined, and unknown are three different stored values. Size the servers for the rush and the disk for a normal day. The next person who reads the row must be able to tell a bank's 'no' from a bank's silence.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapter 1 — Reliable, scalable, maintainable","ddiaBody":"Chapter 1 asks for a percentile, such as p99, because an average latency hides the slow taps. It also separates a fault from a failure: the bank going quiet is a fault, and storing that silence as a decline is a failure you caused. Use the peak to size speed. Use the average to size disk.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour","barBody":"Visa runs authorization, so they grade whether a timeout is stored as unknown and whether a retry places a second hold. Capital One issues the card, so they grade whether a slow dependency still has a defined outcome. Google and Microsoft grade the arithmetic: average for disk, peak for servers, before any product name.","caseBody":"Netflix's Simian Army post is the case for this hour, even though the product is cards. They treated partial failure as a normal input and practiced it. Read the post for that decision, not for the list of monkeys. On an auth path the same decision is: the issuer call will time out, so the contract for that case is part of the design.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/the-netflix-simian-army-16e57fbab116","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — The Netflix Simian Army","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"200 /s","label":"Average auth attempts"},{"value":"2,000 /s","label":"Peak auth attempts"},{"value":"150 ms","label":"p99 budget at the terminal"},{"value":"500 B","label":"Durable auth record"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Average attempts","200/s. Compute the day's records below.","Disk and retention"],["Peak attempts","2,000/s","Fleet, connections, issuer timeouts"],["Hot retention","18 months online, 7 years archive","Storage tiers"],["Issuer timeout","80 ms inside the 150 ms budget","How long you may wait"]],"words":[["Authorization","The fast decision at the terminal. A yes usually places a hold. The shop has not been paid."],["Hold","The available balance drops so the same money cannot be spent again. It is not a payment to the shop."],["Clearing and settlement","Later steps. The shop submits the actual amount, then money moves between banks. Different clock, different design."],["Not this hour","Say those later steps exist. Do not draw them. This 90 minutes is only the decision."],["Declined","The bank said no. That is a recorded answer, not a silence."],["Unknown","The bank did not answer in time, or the answer was lost. You do not know if a hold exists."],["p99 of 150 ms","99 of 100 taps finish within 150 milliseconds. The average can look fine while the slow taps do not."],["Average vs peak","200/s is a normal moment and sizes disk. 2,000/s is a rush and sizes servers. The rush does not last all day."],["Who is grading","Visa and Capital One grade the timeout and the hold. Google and Microsoft stop you if you name a database before the arithmetic."],["Source of truth","The record that wins when a cache and a service disagree."],["Idempotency key","An id for this attempt. The same key on a retry returns the stored outcome. A new key is a new attempt."]],"choices":[{"id":"scope","prompt":"What is in scope for this hour?","sound":"auth-only","staff":"The stronger scope is the authorization decision only. Clearing, settlement, and disputes are named and deferred. Visa interviewers listen for that cut, because those files are a different consistency and a different latency.","options":[{"value":"auth-only","label":"Auth decision only. Name clearing as out of scope.","effect":"You still have time for the 150 ms decision. Clearing is one line under out of scope."},{"value":"full-stack","label":"Auth, clearing, settlement, and disputes in one diagram.","effect":"Four products are on the page. The tap that has a deadline never gets a deep dive."},{"value":"ledger-first","label":"Start from the general ledger and derive auth from it.","effect":"You are designing week 9's ledger in week 1. The terminal is still waiting."}]},{"id":"size","prompt":"Which number sizes the database, and which sizes the fleet?","sound":"split","staff":"Size storage from average daily volume times retention times replication. Size the serving fleet and the issuer connection pool from the peak. Using peak times 86,400 for disk overbuilds storage by about 10x and still does not answer the latency question.","options":[{"value":"split","label":"Average for storage. Peak for fleet and latency.","effect":"Disk follows a normal day. Servers follow the rush, so the slow taps still fit in 150 ms."},{"value":"peak-all","label":"Peak QPS times seconds-per-day for both disk and fleet.","effect":"You buy about ten times too much disk. You still have not said whether the tail fits in 150 ms."},{"value":"avg-all","label":"Average QPS for both, with a note that peak is rare.","effect":"Disk is fine. The fleet is sized for a quiet hour, and the holiday rush misses the deadline."}]},{"id":"truth","prompt":"What is the source of truth for the auth decision?","sound":"decision-log","staff":"The source of truth is a durable decision record: one row per attempt, with the idempotency key, the outcome, and the issuer reference. A cache of recent decisions is allowed. The cache is not the record Capital One or Visa will audit.","options":[{"value":"decision-log","label":"A durable decision row. Cache is optional and rebuildable.","effect":"A retry can find the first outcome. An auditor can tell approved, declined, and unknown apart."},{"value":"cache","label":"The cache of recent approvals is the record.","effect":"A cache eviction looks like 'this tap never happened.' A retry can place a second hold."},{"value":"issuer","label":"The issuer is the only store. You keep nothing.","effect":"When the bank is slow, you have no row to replay. Every retry is a new attempt."}]}],"failureTitle":"Issuer timeout at 80 ms, then the terminal retries","failureBody":"The issuer does not answer. Your process may have received an approval and failed before writing it, or the issuer may never have seen the request. The terminal sends the same attempt again. Say what the cardholder can observe, and what must not happen to the hold.","reviewIntro":"A hire-level answer at Visa or Capital One is a small sync path and a boring audit row. Google and Microsoft still want the arithmetic before the diagram.","review":["Sync path: authenticate the terminal, read the decision log by idempotency key, call the issuer inside the remaining budget, and commit the decision row before you ack. Async path: notifications, analytics, and the handoff toward clearing. The caller does not wait for those.","On timeout, return 'unknown' or a decline that you have recorded, and require the same idempotency key on retry. Placing a second hold is the defect. Declining when you are unsure spends availability to protect correctness, and you should say that tradeoff out loud.","Rough storage: 17 million records/day times 500 bytes is about 8.5 GB/day before indexes and replication. Eighteen months hot is a few terabytes, not a special distributed database. The hard part is the 80 ms dependency, not the bytes."],"rubric":[["Scope","Auth only, clearing named and deferred"],["Estimates","Peak for p99, average for bytes, arithmetic shown"],["Source of truth","Durable decision row, cache rebuildable"],["Diagram","Issuer call is sync. Clearing handoff is async."],["Deep dive","The timeout branch, not the happy path"],["Failure","Same key replays one hold. A new key is a new attempt."],["Tradeoff","Decline or unknown on timeout, said explicitly"]]},{"file":"week-02-charge-idempotency","weekLabel":"Week 2 · Idempotency","title":"Idempotent card charge","minutes":"90 min","company":"Visa / Revolut","timed":false,"brief":"Design the charge endpoint so a client timeout cannot debit the account twice. Then run the code lab and match your story to what the program prints.","goal":"","idea":"At-least-once delivery plus an idempotent handler is the practical version of exactly-once side effects. The key and the debit are one transaction. A new key is a new business attempt.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapter 7 — Transactions","ddiaBody":"Chapter 7 is about atomicity across the rows that must agree. Here those rows are the idempotency key and the debit. If they commit separately, chapter 8 shows up: a crash between them looks like a retryable failure.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Visa and Revolut","barBody":"Visa and Revolut fail this interview if a retry can create a second hold. Microsoft would also ask for the key's scope and TTL. Write the scope down before you pick Redis versus Postgres.","caseBody":"The Simian Army post is about killing instances on purpose. A charge handler will be killed between two writes in production without anyone's permission. Design the transaction so that kill is boring.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/the-netflix-simian-army-16e57fbab116","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — The Netflix Simian Army","codeLab":"From the repo root, run python3 labs/idempotency/charge.py. Read the four outcomes. Your commit should say which outcome your design allows.","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"300 /s","label":"Average charges"},{"value":"1,500 /s","label":"Peak charges"},{"value":"2 s","label":"Client timeout"},{"value":"24 h","label":"Key retention, at least"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Key owner","The client creates it, one per user attempt","Retry identity"],["Key storage","Same database transaction as the debit","Atomicity"],["Stored payload","The status and the body you will replay","Stable retries"],["Decline","Stored and replayed for that key","No accidental later approval"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"scope","prompt":"What is the idempotency key's scope?","sound":"attempt","staff":"Scope the key to one client and one endpoint, unique per attempt. A global random key with no owner makes support blind. Reusing one key for every charge from that client collapses distinct purchases.","options":[{"value":"attempt","label":"Client-generated, unique per attempt, scoped to that client and endpoint."},{"value":"daily","label":"One key per customer per day."},{"value":"server","label":"The server mints a new key on every request, including retries."}]},{"id":"store","prompt":"Where is the key stored relative to the debit?","sound":"same-tx","staff":"Same transaction as the debit and the stored response. A cache-only key disappears on failover, and the retry debits again. The code lab's crash path is this bug.","options":[{"value":"same-tx","label":"Same database transaction as the debit and the response."},{"value":"cache-first","label":"Write Redis, then the database, as two steps."},{"value":"after","label":"Debit first. Insert the key after the response is sent."}]},{"id":"replay","prompt":"What does a retry return?","sound":"stored","staff":"Replay the stored status and body, including a decline. Recomputing can approve a retry that previously declined, or decline a retry that previously approved, once balances move.","options":[{"value":"stored","label":"Replay the stored result for that key."},{"value":"recompute","label":"Run the charge logic again and hope it matches."},{"value":"always-ok","label":"Return 200 with a new id so the client stops retrying."}]}],"failureTitle":"Client times out at 2 seconds. The debit commits at 2.1 seconds.","failureBody":"The client does not see the approval. It retries. Walk both versions: same key, and a brand-new key the client minted because it assumed the first call failed.","reviewIntro":"The code lab is the interview. If your story disagrees with the printout, change the story.","review":["Sync path: insert-or-read the key, and in that same transaction apply the debit only if this is the first time. Return the stored result. Async path: receipt notification, fraud features, ledger fan-out. Those consumers carry their own keys.","A timeout is an unknown outcome. Minting a new key turns one purchase into two. Revolut support will see two approvals and one angry customer. Visa will see two holds."],"rubric":[["Scope","One charge attempt, not the whole payments platform"],["Estimates","Peak charge rate and key retention"],["Source of truth","The charge row plus its key, one transaction"],["Diagram","Sync debit. Async receipt."],["Deep dive","Crash between debit and key"],["Failure","Same key replays. New key is a second purchase."],["Tradeoff","You store responses you could have recomputed, for stability"]]},{"file":"week-02-ledger-vs-feed","weekLabel":"Week 2 · Consistency","title":"Ledger versus activity feed","minutes":"90 min","company":"Monzo / Revolut","timed":false,"brief":"The same app shows a balance used to approve a card, and a home screen of recent activity. Those two screens are not allowed to use the same freshness rule.","goal":"Decide which read must see the latest commit, which read may be a few seconds behind, and when you are willing to tell the customer the debit succeeded.","idea":"A copy being a few seconds behind is normal. Using that copy to approve a card is the bug. The home screen may lag. The authorization may not.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 5 and 9 — Replication and consistency","ddiaBody":"Chapter 5: after a write, followers catch up. That gap is replication lag. Chapter 9: a linearizable read must see the latest commit. Map the card check to that read. Map the home screen to a follower that may lag.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour","barBody":"Monzo and Revolut ask whether a customer can spend the same money twice because a copy was behind. Google or Microsoft also ask which copy the read hits, and what you do when the primary cannot be reached.","caseBody":"Uber's H3 post is about indexing the map into hexes, not about replication. Read it for one habit only: they picked a representation that matches the access pattern, and they accepted what that representation is bad at. Then make the same kind of cut here. A card check has to see the latest debit. A history list can be behind. Driver assignment in their marketplace is the 'must be fresh' side. A rider's old trips list is the 'may lag' side.","caseUrl":"https://www.uber.com/blog/h3/","caseLink":"Uber Engineering — H3, the hexagonal geospatial index","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"4M","label":"Accounts"},{"value":"500 /s","label":"Peak balance checks"},{"value":"50 ms","label":"p99 balance read"},{"value":"5 s","label":"Feed lag you can explain"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Accounts","4 million","How many balance rows"],["Peak balance checks","500/s. A quiet hour is about 50/s.","You decide which rate sizes disk"],["Balance deadline","50 ms p99","How fresh the read must be"],["Feed lag you can explain","5 seconds","What the home screen may show"]],"words":[["Primary","The copy that takes the write. A balance check for a card reads this, or something just as fresh."],["Replica or follower","A copy that receives the write afterward. It can be seconds behind. Fine for a feed. Wrong for approving a card."],["Linearizable read","A read that is guaranteed to see the latest committed write. Chapter 9's name for 'not a stale copy.'"],["Derived feed","A list built from the ledger. If it is wrong, you rebuild it. It is not a second balance."],["Ack","The moment you tell the client the debit succeeded. If you say that before a durable copy exists, a crash can lose the debit."]],"choices":[{"id":"ledger","prompt":"What does a balance check for a card read?","sound":"linearizable","staff":"A balance check used for authorization reads the primary, or a linearizable leader, often under a lock or a conditional update on that account. An async replica is how the same money gets spent twice. That is a double-spend bug.","options":[{"value":"linearizable","label":"The primary, or a read that is guaranteed to see the latest commit.","effect":"A second tap sees the first debit. The customer cannot spend the same money twice."},{"value":"eventual","label":"Any copy. A second of lag is acceptable for a balance.","effect":"The second tap can be approved against money that was already spent. That is a double spend."},{"value":"cache","label":"A 5 second balance cache in front of the database.","effect":"Approvals keep succeeding until the cache expires, against a balance that already changed."}]},{"id":"feed","prompt":"What does the home screen read?","sound":"lag-ok","staff":"The feed is derived. A few seconds of lag is acceptable if a pull-to-refresh can read the primary and if the feed can be rebuilt from the ledger. Do not let the feed become a second ledger.","options":[{"value":"lag-ok","label":"A follower. Rebuildable from the ledger. Refresh reads the primary.","effect":"The home screen can be a few seconds late. The card check is unaffected. You can rebuild the list."},{"value":"same","label":"The same freshest read as the card check, for every home-screen view.","effect":"Correct, and expensive. Every scroll competes with card approvals for the primary."},{"value":"feed-truth","label":"The feed is the balance. The ledger is built from the feed.","effect":"A late or duplicated feed event becomes the money. You now have two ledgers, and they will disagree."}]},{"id":"ack","prompt":"When do you tell the customer the debit succeeded?","sound":"sync-replica","staff":"First name the durability you promised. If an acknowledged debit must survive the primary dying, wait for a synchronous copy or a quorum. If a small loss window is acceptable, acknowledging the primary alone is a real choice, and you say what the customer sees when that debit disappears. The copy is not universally required. The contract comes first.","options":[{"value":"sync-replica","label":"After the primary and one copy that must confirm have both stored it.","effect":"A crash of the primary does not forget the debit. The customer waits a little longer for that copy."},{"value":"primary-only","label":"After the primary stores it. Copies catch up later.","effect":"The answer is faster. If the primary dies before a copy catches up, the debit you confirmed can disappear."},{"value":"client-ok","label":"As soon as the request is accepted. Store it afterward.","effect":"The customer sees success, then a crash drops the debit. Support has to explain a payment that never posted."}]}],"failureTitle":"The customer pays, the follower is behind, they pay again","failureBody":"A card authorization commits on the primary. The home screen reads a follower that does not have the debit yet. The customer thinks the payment failed and submits it again. Describe what each submit does to the balance.","reviewIntro":"Two products, two read models. Say that sentence before you draw boxes.","review":["Sync path for spend: lock or conditional update on the account, commit with a sync replica, return the decision. Async path: update the derived feed. The feed is allowed to miss the debit for a few seconds.","The second submit is safe only with the same idempotency key. If the client minted a new key because the screen looked stale, you have a product bug and a ledger bug. Show a pending state from the primary, not from the follower, after the customer hits pay."],"rubric":[["Scope","Spend path and home feed are different reads"],["Estimates","Peak balance checks sized separately from feed reads"],["Source of truth","Ledger primary. Feed is derived."],["Diagram","Sync commit for the debit. Async projection for the feed."],["Deep dive","Which replica a balance check is allowed to hit"],["Failure","Stale feed cannot authorize a second spend"],["Tradeoff","Feed lag accepted. Auth lag rejected."]]},{"file":"week-03-cache-boundaries","weekLabel":"Week 3 · Derived data","title":"What you may cache","minutes":"90 min","company":"Google / Microsoft, with a Capital One constraint","timed":false,"brief":"Three reads land on your desk: Netflix-style title metadata, an Uber-style driver location used for dispatch, and an available balance used for a card auth. Decide what a cache is allowed to answer.","goal":"","idea":"A cache is derived data. If it disappears, you rebuild it from the source. If a wrong answer moves money or assigns a driver, it is not a cache. It is a second source of truth you did not mean to build.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapter 12 — Derived data","ddiaBody":"Chapter 12 treats indexes, caches, and materialized views as data you can rebuild from the source of truth. The design move is to name the source, the lag you allow, and the decision that must not use the lagging copy.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Google or Microsoft, Capital One on the balance","barBody":"Google and Microsoft want the invalidation story and the stampede story. Capital One ends the interview if an authorization can succeed on a stale balance. Say the three reads out loud and give them different answers.","caseBody":"Netflix can cache catalog metadata because a slightly stale synopsis does not double-charge anyone. Their personalization post is about recomputing derived views on a schedule and online. Uber cannot dispatch from a location that is half a minute old. Read the H3 post as a spatial index for marketplace analysis, not as permission to cache a location and not as their assignment algorithm.","caseUrl":"https://www.uber.com/blog/h3/","caseLink":"Uber Engineering — H3","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"20 ms","label":"Metadata cache hit target"},{"value":"2 s","label":"Max location age for dispatch"},{"value":"0 s","label":"Staleness allowed on an auth balance"},{"value":"30 s","label":"Catalog TTL you may defend"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Catalog metadata","Cache, TTL or event invalidation","Read scale"],["Driver location","Live store, single-digit seconds","Dispatch correctness"],["Available balance","Ledger primary or linearizable read","Auth correctness"],["Cache miss","Single-flight rebuild from source","Stampede"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"catalog","prompt":"Can title metadata be cached?","sound":"yes-ttl","staff":"Yes. A short TTL or an invalidation event is enough. The source is the catalog store. A stale poster is a product bug, not a ledger bug. Say the TTL.","options":[{"value":"yes-ttl","label":"Cache it. Name the TTL or the invalidation event."},{"value":"never","label":"Never cache metadata. Read the primary on every homepage hit."},{"value":"forever","label":"Cache until the process restarts."}]},{"id":"location","prompt":"Can dispatch use a 30 second location cache?","sound":"no","staff":"No. Dispatch reads a live location with an age bound you can say, on the order of a couple of seconds. A 30 second cache assigns a driver who has already turned the corner. Uber's index exists so the fresh point is cheap to query, not so you can skip freshness.","options":[{"value":"no","label":"Live location with a small age bound. The cache is not the dispatch input."},{"value":"yes","label":"30 second cache. Dispatch is approximate anyway."},{"value":"either","label":"Cache locations the same way as catalog metadata."}]},{"id":"balance","prompt":"Can an authorization use a cached balance?","sound":"primary","staff":"No. Capital One and Visa authorize against the ledger's current available balance, under a lock or a conditional update. A cache can sit on the home screen. It cannot approve the card.","options":[{"value":"primary","label":"Authorization reads the ledger, not the cache."},{"value":"five","label":"A 5 second balance cache is close enough."},{"value":"either-bal","label":"Cache balances the same way as catalog metadata."}]}],"failureTitle":"Two 80 dollar auths both see a cached 100 dollar balance","failureBody":"The cache says 100 dollars. Two authorizations arrive close together, both pass the cache, and both are approved. The ledger, if anyone had read it, had 100 dollars total. Write the sequence that prevents this, and what the home screen is still allowed to show.","reviewIntro":"Three reads, three policies. The interview is whether you refuse to unify them.","review":["Catalog: cache in front of the catalog store, single-flight on miss, invalidate on publish. Location: a live geospatial index, return the age, refuse to dispatch on a stale point. Balance: ledger primary inside the auth transaction.","The home screen may show a cached balance if it is labeled and if pull-to-refresh reads the primary. The card path may not. Chapter 12: when the copies disagree, the source wins, and the copy must be rebuildable."],"rubric":[["Scope","Three reads named, three policies"],["Estimates","Hit latency, location age, auth staleness"],["Source of truth","Catalog store, location store, ledger. Cache is none of them."],["Diagram","Cache on the read that can be wrong. Not on the read that moves money."],["Deep dive","The double auth on a stale balance"],["Failure","Second auth sees the first hold"],["Tradeoff","More ledger reads on the auth path to keep the invariant"]]},{"file":"week-03-partitioning","weekLabel":"Week 3 · Partitioning","title":"Partition payments and features","minutes":"90 min","company":"Google / Capital One","timed":false,"brief":"You are splitting two tables that do not share an access pattern: payment postings, and online feature rows for a fraud model. Pick keys that make the critical read hit one partition, and say what you will do about a hot key.","goal":"","idea":"Partition for the query you must answer under the latency budget. A key that spreads load and misses the query is a failed partition. A key that serves the query and creates one hot shard needs a written plan, not a shrug.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapter 6 — Partitioning","ddiaBody":"Chapter 6 is keys, skew, and hot spots. Rebalancing by hash mod N makes every growth event a full move. Fixed partitions that you move as units age better. Map that onto account id versus merchant id.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Google and Capital One","barBody":"Google asks why this key, what the hot key is, and how you rebalance. Capital One asks whether a balance check stays on one partition. A feature read for fraud has the same shape: one entity, one row, one deadline.","caseBody":"Uber's H3 post is a spatial index for marketplace analysis, including pricing and dispatch. A nearby query that hits a handful of cells is a design inspired by that index, not a documented production algorithm. Your payment table needs the same discipline for a different key: the key follows the question, and a hot key needs a plan.","caseUrl":"https://www.uber.com/blog/h3/","caseLink":"Uber Engineering — H3","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"40k /s","label":"Peak posting writes"},{"value":"1 account","label":"Partitions touched by a balance check"},{"value":"1 entity","label":"Partitions touched by a feature read"},{"value":"20%","label":"Writes a viral merchant can concentrate"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Balance check","All postings for one account","Partition by account id"],["Merchant report","Secondary index or a separate table","Do not steal the balance key"],["Feature read","One entity id at score time","Partition features by entity"],["Rebalance","Move a fixed partition, not hash mod N","Growth"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"pay-key","prompt":"What is the partition key for postings?","sound":"account","staff":"Account id. The critical read is the balance of one account, and it should hit one partition. Merchant id scatters one cardholder across the cluster and makes the auth slow. Put merchant analytics in another projection.","options":[{"value":"account","label":"Account id. Merchant analytics live elsewhere."},{"value":"merchant","label":"Merchant id, because large merchants dominate traffic."},{"value":"random","label":"Random uuid, for perfectly even disks."}]},{"id":"feat-key","prompt":"What is the partition key for online features?","sound":"entity","staff":"Entity id, the card or the account you are scoring. Model id would spray one decision across every model partition. Capital One's scorer has a deadline. One read.","options":[{"value":"entity","label":"Entity id, so scoring is a point read."},{"value":"model","label":"Model id, so each model owns a shard."},{"value":"time","label":"Event time, so writes append nicely."}]},{"id":"rebalance","prompt":"How do you add capacity?","sound":"fixed","staff":"Pre-split into many fixed partitions and move partitions onto new nodes. Hash mod N reshuffles every key when N changes. Chapter 6 spends real pages on this, because teams rediscover it in production.","options":[{"value":"fixed","label":"Many fixed partitions. Move partitions to new nodes."},{"value":"modn","label":"hash(key) mod N, and increase N when disks fill."},{"value":"manual","label":"Hand-split the hot account when someone complains."}]}],"failureTitle":"One merchant is 20 percent of writes on a single posting partition","failureBody":"You partitioned by account, and one merchant account is huge, or a celebrity account receives a burst. The partition is hot. The rest of the cluster is idle. Write the mitigation that does not break the single-partition balance check for ordinary accounts.","reviewIntro":"Say the query first, then the key. Hot keys get a plan, not a different key for everyone.","review":["Ordinary accounts stay on account id so authorization touches one partition and can lock it. A known hot account can be isolated, or its postings sub-split by time with a small index of segments, so the balance read fans out to a bounded set you can name.","Features stay on entity id. Training scans are a batch job over a snapshot, not the online key. Do not partition the online store for the batch job's convenience. Chapter 6: skew is a first-class input."],"rubric":[["Scope","Two tables, two keys, stated"],["Estimates","Peak writes and the one-partition read"],["Source of truth","Postings for money. Feature store for scores, not for money."],["Diagram","Point read path versus the async analytic projection"],["Deep dive","The hot account plan"],["Failure","A hot key cannot double-approve by skipping the lock"],["Tradeoff","Uneven load accepted so the critical read stays local"]]},{"file":"week-04-retries-and-poison","weekLabel":"Week 4 · Partial failure","title":"Retries and poison messages","minutes":"90 min","company":"Revolut / Microsoft","timed":false,"brief":"Two bugs are in production. A charge timeout is treated as 'not charged,' so the client sends a new key. A malformed settlement message retries forever and blocks one account's partition.","goal":"","idea":"You often cannot tell a slow success from a failure. Retries need a bound, a key, and jitter. A message that will never succeed must leave the partition and sit where a human can see it, without being applied.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapter 8 — The trouble with distributed systems","ddiaBody":"Chapter 8 is timeouts, unreliable networks, and the pause that looks like death. The design move is to name the unknown outcome, and to stop a single bad message from stalling the log.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Revolut and Microsoft","barBody":"Revolut grades the double charge. Microsoft grades whether you bounded the retry and what the caller sees. A poison message that you skip silently is a lost settlement. A poison message that you retry forever is an outage.","caseBody":"The Simian Army post is the cultural version of chapter 8: instances die, networks delay, and the design still has to say what happens. Read it for the decision to practice failure, then write the retry budget for a charge.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/the-netflix-simian-army-16e57fbab116","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — The Netflix Simian Army","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"3","label":"Charge attempts, including the first"},{"value":"Full jitter","label":"Backoff between attempts"},{"value":"5","label":"Settlement deliveries before quarantine"},{"value":"0","label":"Poison messages applied to the ledger"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Charge timeout","Unknown outcome, same idempotency key","Prevents a second debit"],["Backoff","Bounded, with jitter","Prevents a retry storm"],["Poison settlement","Quarantine after 5, alert a human","Unblocks the partition"],["Quarantine","Not applied. Visible in an ops queue.","Audit"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"charge-retry","prompt":"The issuer timed out. What does the client do?","sound":"same-key","staff":"Retry a small number of times with the same idempotency key and jitter. A new key is a second charge. Immediate tight-loop retries amplify the outage. Chapter 8: the timeout did not tell you the fate of the first call.","options":[{"value":"same-key","label":"Bounded retries, jitter, same key."},{"value":"new-key","label":"New key, because the first call failed."},{"value":"forever","label":"Retry immediately until something answers."}]},{"id":"poison","prompt":"A settlement message fails validation every time. What happens?","sound":"quarantine","staff":"After a small number of attempts, move it to a quarantine and alert. Do not apply it. Do not retry it on the hot path forever. Revolut cannot skip it silently, because the capture would have no settlement. A human or a repair tool resolves the quarantine with an audited action.","options":[{"value":"quarantine","label":"Quarantine, alert, leave the ledger unchanged."},{"value":"forever","label":"Retry forever so no message is ever dropped."},{"value":"skip","label":"Log it and skip so the partition keeps moving."}]},{"id":"unknown","prompt":"How do you classify a timeout?","sound":"unknown","staff":"Unknown, not failure. Failure means you know the debit did not commit. Unknown means you must reconcile or replay the same key. Microsoft interviewers listen for that distinction. It is the whole chapter.","options":[{"value":"unknown","label":"Unknown. Reconcile or replay the same key."},{"value":"failure","label":"Failure. The debit did not happen."},{"value":"success","label":"Success. Show the customer a receipt anyway."}]}],"failureTitle":"Both bugs at once","failureBody":"A charge times out and the mobile client generates a new idempotency key. Meanwhile a poison settlement for a different account is retrying so fast that the worker pool is saturated. Write the client rule and the worker rule separately.","reviewIntro":"Bound the retry. Preserve the key. Park the poison. Do not apply a message you cannot parse.","review":["Charge path: three attempts, full jitter, same key, then show 'unknown' and a support reference that maps to the key. The server stores whatever outcome it did commit, so the third attempt replays it.","Settlement path: five deliveries, then quarantine. The account partition moves on. An operator repairs or posts an explicit reversing entry. Skipping the message loses money movement. Looping it takes the worker down. Chapter 8's partial failure needs an operator path, not only a retry loop."],"rubric":[["Scope","Client retry policy and consumer poison policy"],["Estimates","Attempt budget and quarantine threshold"],["Source of truth","Charge row for the attempt. Ledger unchanged by poison."],["Diagram","Sync charge with a budget. Async quarantine."],["Deep dive","Why a timeout is unknown"],["Failure","No second charge. No silent drop. No infinite loop."],["Tradeoff","A human in the quarantine, instead of a stuck partition"]]},{"file":"week-04-settlement-outbox","weekLabel":"Week 4 · Streams","title":"Settlement outbox","minutes":"90 min","company":"JPMorgan Chase / Visa","timed":false,"brief":"When a capture commits, a settlement instruction must reach a downstream worker. The database and the queue do not share a transaction. Design the handoff, then run the code lab.","goal":"","idea":"Dual writes drift. The outbox makes the database the source of the event. The queue is a derived stream. Consumers assume at-least-once delivery and apply the instruction once.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapter 11 — Stream processing","ddiaBody":"Chapter 11 treats a log as the transport of derived data. The outbox is how you get a business commit into that log without pretending two systems can commit atomically. Consumers are idempotent because delivery is at least once.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: JPMorgan Chase and Visa","barBody":"JPMorgan Chase wants the instruction to exist after a crash, and an audit of when it was published. Visa wants the settlement file and the capture to be matchable later. Losing the message and keeping the capture is a reconciliation incident.","caseBody":"Netflix's personalization systems move derived data from an offline computation to an online store. The lesson to steal is the direction of derivation, not the model. Your queue is derived from the settlement row. The settlement row is not derived from the queue.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/system-architectures-for-personalization-and-recommendation-e081aa94b5d8","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — personalization architectures","codeLab":"From the repo root, run python3 labs/outbox/outbox.py. The naive path loses the event. The outbox path delivers it once after a restart.","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"200 /s","label":"Average captures"},{"value":"2,000 /s","label":"Peak captures"},{"value":"1 row","label":"Outbox insert per capture"},{"value":"0","label":"Lost instructions you can defend"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Business commit","Capture row and outbox row, one transaction","Durability"],["Publisher","Drains unpublished outbox rows","Restart behavior"],["Ordering","Partition the stream by account id","Per-account order"],["Consumer","Idempotent apply by instruction id","At-least-once delivery"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"handoff","prompt":"How does the instruction leave the database?","sound":"outbox","staff":"Outbox row in the same transaction as the capture. Publish after commit. A write to the database followed by a separate queue publish loses the instruction when the process dies in the gap. The code lab prints that loss.","options":[{"value":"outbox","label":"Outbox row in the same transaction. Publisher drains it."},{"value":"dual","label":"Commit the capture, then publish to the queue."},{"value":"queue-first","label":"Publish first, then commit the capture if the publish worked."}]},{"id":"consumer","prompt":"What delivery semantic do you design for?","sound":"at-least-once","staff":"At-least-once delivery, idempotent consumer. Exactly-once across a database and a queue is the claim teams write down and then fail. Store the instruction id on the settlement apply.","options":[{"value":"at-least-once","label":"At-least-once, and the apply is idempotent."},{"value":"exactly","label":"Exactly-once, so the consumer can apply blindly."},{"value":"at-most","label":"At-most-once. Dropping a settlement is safer."}]},{"id":"order","prompt":"What ordering do you promise?","sound":"per-account","staff":"Order per account, by partitioning the stream on account id. Global order is expensive and unused. No order at all lets a refund apply before the capture for the same account.","options":[{"value":"per-account","label":"Per account, via the partition key."},{"value":"global","label":"One global partition so every instruction is ordered."},{"value":"none","label":"No ordering promise."}]}],"failureTitle":"Process crash after the business commit, before the publish","failureBody":"The capture is durable. The publisher has not run. Describe what a restart does, and what a second delivery of the same instruction does to the downstream settlement.","reviewIntro":"If you cannot point at the row that survives the crash, you do not have a handoff.","review":["Sync path: commit capture plus outbox. The caller can be acked there. Async path: publisher marks the outbox row published only after the queue accepts it. If the process dies after publish and before the mark, the restart publishes again, and the consumer's idempotency key makes that boring.","JPMorgan Chase will ask for an audit of unpublished rows older than a threshold. That query is your operations story. Visa will ask how this instruction is matched to clearing later. The instruction id is the join key."],"rubric":[["Scope","The handoff, not the whole card network"],["Estimates","Peak captures and one outbox row each"],["Source of truth","Capture and outbox in the database. Queue is derived."],["Diagram","Sync commit. Async publish and apply."],["Deep dive","Restart and duplicate delivery"],["Failure","No lost instruction. No double apply."],["Tradeoff","An extra row and a publisher, instead of a phantom distributed transaction"]]},{"file":"week-05-notification-drill","weekLabel":"Week 5 · Timed drill","title":"Payment notification drill","minutes":"45 min drill","company":"Microsoft / Monzo","timed":true,"brief":"Closed book. Forty-five minutes. Design notifications for payment events. Write the seven notes on this page. Those notes are the answer.","goal":"","idea":"A notification is a derived stream of a ledger event. Delivery is at least once. The user-visible copy for a charge must not fan out into four contradictory texts.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapter 11 — Streams and consumers","ddiaBody":"Chapter 11: consumers see duplicates and must be idempotent. Map that onto one logical payment event and three channels.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Microsoft and Monzo","barBody":"Microsoft grades fan-out, retries, and per-channel failure. Monzo grades whether a customer can miss a payment receipt or receive a confusing duplicate. The debrief stays closed until you ask for it.","caseBody":"Debrief, only after you commit. Fan out per user from a durable payment event, with an idempotency key of user plus event id plus channel. Provider timeouts are unknown deliveries: retry the same provider id, and let the provider dedupe where it can. A missing receipt is repaired by the ledger event still being in the outbox. A fourth SMS is a bug in the key.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/the-netflix-simian-army-16e57fbab116","caseLink":"After the drill: partial failure, Simian Army post","codeLab":"","sealed":"Interview prompt. 20 million users, peak 5,000 sends per second, channels are push, SMS, and email. A payment receipt should show up within 5 seconds at p99. SMS for a charge must not arrive four times because a provider timed out. You have 45 minutes. Write the seven moves in the journal. Do not look up the case.","numbers":[{"value":"20M","label":"Users"},{"value":"5,000 /s","label":"Peak sends"},{"value":"5 s","label":"p99 payment receipt"},{"value":"3","label":"Channels"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Users","20 million","Device token storage"],["Peak","5,000 sends/s","Worker fleet"],["Receipt SLO","5 seconds p99","Sync versus async cut"],["Channels","Push, SMS, email","Per-channel idempotency"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"source","prompt":"What creates a notification?","sound":"ledger-event","staff":"A durable payment event, via the outbox. The API handler does not call SMS vendors on the card path. Monzo wants the receipt to exist even if the request process died after the commit.","options":[{"value":"ledger-event","label":"A durable ledger or payment event."},{"value":"handler","label":"The request handler calls the vendors before it returns."},{"value":"client","label":"The phone sends the SMS locally."}]},{"id":"fanout","prompt":"How do you fan out?","sound":"per-user","staff":"Per user, per event, per channel, with a stored send record. A global broadcast topic is the wrong shape for a receipt. Microsoft expects the send record.","options":[{"value":"per-user","label":"Per user, event, and channel, with a send record."},{"value":"broadcast","label":"One broadcast to all channels for all users."},{"value":"poll","label":"The app polls the ledger and notifies locally only."}]},{"id":"dedupe","prompt":"The SMS provider times out. What do you retry?","sound":"same-id","staff":"The same send id. A new id is how the customer gets a second 'you were charged' text. If the provider cannot dedupe, your send record still must, and the copy should be stable.","options":[{"value":"same-id","label":"Retry the same send id."},{"value":"new-id","label":"New id on each timeout so the provider accepts it."},{"value":"give-up","label":"One try only. Timeouts are dropped."}]}],"failureTitle":"Provider timeout. The user gets four charge texts.","failureBody":"The worker's call to the SMS provider hits its deadline. The worker tries again. The user eventually shows a screenshot with four texts for one coffee. Write the id that should have made those retries one send, and what you do if the provider actually delivered and then timed out on the ack.","reviewIntro":"This is the debrief. Compare it with the journal you committed, and mark the moves you skipped.","review":["Sync path: commit the payment. Do not wait for SMS. Async path: outbox to a notifier, send record per channel, provider call with a stable id, retry with jitter, quarantine after a bound.","Unknown delivery is still one logical send. Support tooling looks up the payment id and shows the send attempts. Monzo cares about the copy. Microsoft cares about the fan-out data model. Both are in the same design."],"rubric":[["Scope","Receipts, not a general campaign platform"],["Estimates","5,000 sends/s and the 5 second p99"],["Source of truth","Payment event. Send record is derived."],["Diagram","Async after the payment commit"],["Deep dive","Provider timeout and duplicate texts"],["Failure","Same send id. Bounded retries."],["Tradeoff","Receipt can be a few seconds late. It cannot be a second charge story."]]},{"file":"week-05-rate-limiter","weekLabel":"Week 5 · Limits","title":"Rate limiter for a money endpoint","minutes":"90 min","company":"Google / Microsoft / Capital One","timed":false,"brief":"Put a limiter in front of a card-testing route and, separately, in front of a fraud-model route. The two limits do not fail the same way when the limiter store is down.","goal":"","idea":"A limiter is a budget with a race. Fixed windows burst at the boundary. Clocks skew. A central store is a dependency. Decide, before the outage, whether this route fails open or fails closed.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 6 and 8 — Hot keys and partial failure","ddiaBody":"Chapter 6 shows up as a hot counter key. Chapter 8 shows up when that counter store times out and you must choose a behavior without a perfect view of the count.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Google, Microsoft, and Capital One","barBody":"Google and Microsoft ask for the algorithm, the race, and the multi-node story. Capital One asks what happens to card-testing traffic when Redis is down. Fail-open on that route is a fraud incident.","caseBody":"You do not need a Netflix post to see a limiter. Use the Simian Army stance: the counter store will be unreachable. Write the failure mode next to the algorithm, not in an appendix.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/the-netflix-simian-army-16e57fbab116","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — The Netflix Simian Army","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"5 /min","label":"Card attempts per customer"},{"value":"200 /s","label":"Model route, per client"},{"value":"1 key","label":"Counter per customer, not per IP"},{"value":"Closed","label":"Card route if the limiter store is down"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Card-testing route","Hard cap, fail closed","Fraud loss"],["Model route","Soft cap, local token bucket plus a sync budget","GPU cost"],["Identity","Customer or API client, not IP alone","Shared NATs"],["Window","Token bucket or sliding window","Boundary burst"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"algo","prompt":"Which algorithm do you defend for the card route?","sound":"bucket","staff":"Token bucket or sliding window. A fixed window allows nearly two budgets at the boundary. Say that edge if you still pick a fixed window. Google will ask.","options":[{"value":"bucket","label":"Token bucket or sliding window, and say where the counter lives."},{"value":"fixed","label":"Fixed one-minute window. Simple is the goal."},{"value":"none","label":"The issuer will rate-limit. You do not."}]},{"id":"down","prompt":"The limiter store times out. What does each route do?","sound":"split-fail","staff":"Card-testing fails closed: a short, explicit reject. The model route may fail open with a local cap, because the cost of a few extra scores is smaller than blocking every payment decision, and the ledger still authorizes. Capital One wants this split in one sentence.","options":[{"value":"split-fail","label":"Card route fails closed. Model route has a local fallback cap."},{"value":"open","label":"Both routes fail open. Availability first."},{"value":"closed-all","label":"Both routes fail closed, including healthy payments."}]},{"id":"key","prompt":"What is the limiter key?","sound":"customer","staff":"Customer id or API client id. IP addresses clump real users behind a NAT and miss a distributed card tester. A financial firm will ask who the key is before they ask about Redis.","options":[{"value":"customer","label":"Customer or API client id."},{"value":"ip","label":"Source IP."},{"value":"global","label":"One global counter for the whole route."}]}],"failureTitle":"Clock skew and a Redis blip in the same minute","failureBody":"Two gateway clocks disagree by 20 seconds, so a fixed window admits almost twice the budget. Then Redis stops answering. Card-testing traffic is climbing. Write the admission decision for the card route and for the model route.","reviewIntro":"Algorithm, identity, and the outage behavior. Leave any one out and the interview stalls.","review":["Card route: central token bucket keyed by customer, small burst, fail closed on store timeout, with a metric for rejected-because-unknown. Model route: local buckets that periodically sync, fail to the local cap if the central store is down.","Do not spend the design on Redis versus Memcached. Spend it on the double-admit race and the fail-closed choice. Chapter 8: you will not have a perfect count at the moment you must decide."],"rubric":[["Scope","Two routes, two failure modes"],["Estimates","The actual budgets, in requests per window"],["Source of truth","The counter store for the card cap. Local cap is a fallback."],["Diagram","Sync admission before the handler."],["Deep dive","Store down, card route closed"],["Failure","A retry storm cannot mint extra card attempts"],["Tradeoff","Some good customers rejected when the store is down"]]},{"file":"week-06-activity-feed","weekLabel":"Week 6 · Derived feed","title":"Account activity feed","minutes":"90 min","company":"Revolut / Google","timed":false,"brief":"Design the home-screen activity feed for a bank. This is not a social network. The feed shows one customer's own money movement, and it must be repairable from the ledger.","goal":"","idea":"Fan-out on write into a per-account timeline is the natural shape, because the audience of an account is that account. The timeline is derived. If it disagrees with the ledger, the ledger wins and the timeline is rebuilt.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 11 and 12 — Fan-out and derived data","ddiaBody":"Chapter 11 contrasts fan-out on write with fan-out on read. Chapter 12 says a materialized view is allowed to exist only if you can rebuild it. A bank feed is a materialized view of postings, not a second ledger.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Revolut and Google","barBody":"Revolut fails the interview if the feed can show a posted payment the ledger declined, with no repair. Google wants the read path and the write amplification named. Do not import a celebrity-follower design into a bank.","caseBody":"Netflix's homepage is a derived view rebuilt from offline and online signals. Steal the rebuild, not the ranking. Your feed's source is the ledger. If you cannot replay postings into an empty timeline and match production, the feed has become a source of truth.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/system-architectures-for-personalization-and-recommendation-e081aa94b5d8","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — personalization architectures","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"8M","label":"Accounts"},{"value":"1,000 /s","label":"Peak new postings"},{"value":"50","label":"Items on the first page"},{"value":"2 s","label":"Rebuild target for one account"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Write","One posting, one timeline append","Fan-out is 1, not a follower graph"],["Read","Latest 50 for one account","Partition by account"],["Repair","Replay postings for that account","Derived data"],["Pending","A state the ledger owns, mirrored in the item","No second status machine"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"shape","prompt":"What shape is this feed?","sound":"per-account","staff":"A per-account append log. Fan-out on write is trivial because there is one reader. A Twitter-style follower fan-out solves a problem this product does not have. Google will notice if you recite the social design anyway.","options":[{"value":"per-account","label":"Append to that account's timeline when a posting commits."},{"value":"social","label":"Fan out to followers. Treat payees as followers."},{"value":"scan","label":"Scan the whole posting table on every home open."}]},{"id":"truth","prompt":"If the feed and the ledger disagree, who wins?","sound":"ledger","staff":"The ledger. The feed item stores the posting id and can be deleted or rewritten by a rebuild. Revolut will ask you to say this before they care about Redis versus a table.","options":[{"value":"ledger","label":"Ledger wins. The feed is rebuilt from postings."},{"value":"feed","label":"The feed wins. It is what the customer saw."},{"value":"newest","label":"Whichever write has the later timestamp."}]},{"id":"pending","prompt":"A payment is pending, then declined. What does the feed do?","sound":"mirror","staff":"The item's state follows the ledger transition, keyed by the posting or payment id. Leaving a permanent 'posted' row after a decline is how disputes start. The update is idempotent.","options":[{"value":"mirror","label":"Update the same feed item to the ledger's new state."},{"value":"keep","label":"Keep the original posted row. History should not change."},{"value":"hide","label":"Delete the feed and ask the user to trust the balance only."}]}],"failureTitle":"The feed shows a posted payment the ledger declined","failureBody":"The timeline append happened on the auth hold. The capture then declined, and the projection update was lost. The customer screenshots a posted coffee. The ledger has no capture. Write the repair and the id that makes the update safe to retry.","reviewIntro":"Small fan-out, explicit derivation, one id shared with the ledger.","review":["Sync path: commit the posting. Async path: project one timeline item. Read path: one partition, latest page, no scatter-gather. Rebuild path: replay postings for the account into a new timeline and swap.","The screenshot is a support tool problem as well as a data problem. Store the projection version. Revolut wants the customer-visible state to be explainable from postings. Google wants you to reject the celebrity-fan-out detour in one sentence."],"rubric":[["Scope","One customer's timeline, not a social graph"],["Estimates","Peak postings and a one-partition read"],["Source of truth","Ledger. Feed rebuildable."],["Diagram","Sync posting. Async projection."],["Deep dive","Decline after a pending item"],["Failure","Same payment id updates one item"],["Tradeoff","A projection you must operate, so the home screen is cheap"]]},{"file":"week-06-netflix-personalization","weekLabel":"Week 6 · Batch and stream","title":"Personalization serving path","minutes":"90 min","company":"Google / Microsoft","timed":false,"brief":"Design the online path that picks a row of titles for a homepage. Offline jobs may be heavy. The request has about 100 milliseconds. This is a Google or Microsoft ML-systems question. Netflix is the case, not the employer.","goal":"","idea":"Candidate generation can be batch. Ranking on the request uses fresh context and a latency budget. A cache of the ranked row is allowed only as long as a new play can invalidate it. The catalog scan does not happen on the request.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 10 and 11 — Batch versus stream","ddiaBody":"Chapter 10 is the offline job that can scan everything. Chapter 11 is the fresh signal that cannot wait for tomorrow's job. The serving path is the join of those two, inside a deadline.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Google and Microsoft","barBody":"They want the offline/online cut, the latency budget, and what is cached. A financial firm would ask the same shape for a fraud model: heavy features offline, fresh velocity online, score inside a deadline. Say that parallel if you have time.","caseBody":"Read the Netflix personalization architecture post for the split between offline candidate generation and online ranking. Write down what they precompute, what they do on the request, and what would go wrong if the homepage cache ignored a title the member just finished.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/system-architectures-for-personalization-and-recommendation-e081aa94b5d8","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — personalization architectures","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"100 ms","label":"Online ranking budget"},{"value":"Thousands","label":"Candidates, precomputed"},{"value":"Dozens","label":"Titles after ranking"},{"value":"Hours","label":"Acceptable lag for stable taste"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Candidate generation","Batch or nearline, not the request","Catalog scale"],["Ranking","Online, with session context","The 100 ms budget"],["Fresh signal","What they just played","Stream, not yesterday's batch"],["Cache","Short, or invalidated on play","Stale homepage"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"candidates","prompt":"Where are candidates generated?","sound":"offline","staff":"Offline or nearline, stored per member. Generating candidates by scanning the catalog on the request will miss 100 ms. Google will ask you to put a number on that scan and then abandon it.","options":[{"value":"offline","label":"Precompute candidates. Rank online."},{"value":"scan","label":"Scan the catalog on every homepage request."},{"value":"static","label":"One global top-40 list for everyone."}]},{"id":"fresh","prompt":"A member finishes a title. How fast can the row change?","sound":"session","staff":"Session intent is a stream feature. Stable taste can be hours old. If 'continue watching' only updates in tomorrow's batch, the product looks broken. Chapter 11 is this feature. Chapter 10 is the taste model.","options":[{"value":"session","label":"Stream the play event into the online features. Batch taste can lag."},{"value":"batch-only","label":"Wait for the nightly job."},{"value":"ignore","label":"Ignore the play. The homepage is a daily digest."}]},{"id":"cache","prompt":"How long may you cache the ranked row?","sound":"short","staff":"A short TTL, or invalidate on a new play. A day-long cache saves ranker cost and serves a title they finished. Microsoft will ask what event busts the cache. Name it.","options":[{"value":"short","label":"Short TTL or invalidate on play."},{"value":"day","label":"Cache the row for 24 hours. Ranking is expensive."},{"value":"never","label":"Never cache. Rank from scratch with no budget."}]}],"failureTitle":"They finish a title. The homepage recommends it for six hours.","failureBody":"The play event was produced. The batch job will not run until tonight. A ranked-row cache was filled this morning. Walk the path that removes the title from the row without waiting for the batch job, and name what is still allowed to be stale.","reviewIntro":"Draw two pipelines and one request. Put the deadline on the request only.","review":["Offline: candidates and stable features, versioned, rebuildable. Online: fetch that member's candidates, join session features from the stream, rank inside 100 ms, return a page. Cache key includes a version that a play event can bump.","Cost sits in how often you re-rank and how wide the candidate set is. Freshness sits in the play stream. Say which one you are spending. The same sentence works for a fraud score: batch features for stable risk, a stream for the last ten minutes, a deadline on the auth."],"rubric":[["Scope","Homepage row, not the whole studio pipeline"],["Estimates","100 ms and a candidate-set size"],["Source of truth","Play events and the catalog. The row is derived."],["Diagram","Batch candidates. Stream features. Sync rank."],["Deep dive","Cache invalidation on play"],["Failure","A finished title leaves the row without a new key minted at random"],["Tradeoff","Stable taste can lag. Session intent cannot."]]},{"file":"week-07-location-fanout-drill","weekLabel":"Week 7 · Timed drill","title":"Live location fan-out","minutes":"45 min drill","company":"Google / Microsoft","timed":true,"brief":"Closed book. Forty-five minutes. Fan a driver's live location out to one rider and to dispatch. The journal is the answer.","goal":"","idea":"Location is a stream. Dispatch needs seconds-fresh points. The rider's map can drop intermediate points. Neither consumer is the source. The phone is not the source either, once you have accepted a point into the log.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 5 and 11 — Lag and fan-out","ddiaBody":"Chapter 11 is fan-out of a stream. Chapter 5 is a consumer that lags. Name which consumer is allowed to lag, and which consumer must not act on a stale point.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Google and Microsoft","barBody":"They grade the fan-out, the freshness bound, and what you do when a consumer falls behind. The review stays sealed until the journal is in.","caseBody":"Debrief. Accept points into a log partitioned by driver id. Dispatch reads the latest point per driver from a store updated by the log, and drops anything older than the age bound. The rider subscription gets the latest point on a coarse interval, and dropping points is fine. If dispatch falls behind, it stops matching on stale data rather than assigning from a 30 second-old map. H3 is a reasonable index once you have said the freshness rule.","caseUrl":"https://www.uber.com/blog/h3/","caseLink":"After the drill: Uber H3","codeLab":"","sealed":"Interview prompt. A driver publishes a location about once a second while on a trip. One rider is watching a map. Dispatch is matching new trips using the same stream. Peak is 100,000 drivers online, about 100,000 points per second. Dispatch must ignore points older than 2 seconds. The rider can miss intermediate points. You have 45 minutes. Write the seven moves. Do not open the case.","numbers":[{"value":"100k /s","label":"Peak location points"},{"value":"1 s","label":"Driver publish interval"},{"value":"2 s","label":"Max age for dispatch"},{"value":"1 rider","label":"Subscribers per active trip"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Ingest","100,000 points/s","Partition by driver"],["Dispatch read","Latest point, age under 2 seconds","Match correctness"],["Rider map","Latest point, lossy","Connection count"],["Lag","Dispatch stops using stale points","Partial failure"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"ingest","prompt":"Where do points land first?","sound":"log","staff":"A log partitioned by driver id, then derived stores. Putting the phone's latest UDP packet directly into the matcher with no log makes replay and audit impossible. Microsoft asks how you replay.","options":[{"value":"log","label":"Append-only log by driver, then derived views."},{"value":"matcher","label":"Straight into the matcher memory."},{"value":"database-row","label":"Update one global table with no ordering."}]},{"id":"dispatch","prompt":"Dispatch is 10 seconds behind. What does it do?","sound":"stop","staff":"Stop matching on stale points and shed load, or read only points inside the age bound. Assigning from a lagging map is a safety bug. Google wants the age check at the read, not as a hope.","options":[{"value":"stop","label":"Use only points inside the age bound. Lag means fewer matches, not stale ones."},{"value":"use-stale","label":"Use the latest point you have. A match is a match."},{"value":"buffer","label":"Buffer forever until you catch up, then match the past."}]},{"id":"rider","prompt":"What does the rider connection receive?","sound":"lossy","staff":"The latest point, and it is fine to skip intermediates. Reliable delivery of every point melts the connection and does not help the map. Keep this consumer separate from dispatch so the lossy one cannot weaken the strict one.","options":[{"value":"lossy","label":"Latest point, drop intermediates."},{"value":"exactly","label":"Every point, exactly once, in order, on the socket."},{"value":"poll-minute","label":"The app polls once a minute."}]}],"failureTitle":"The dispatch consumer lags. The rider socket is fine.","failureBody":"A deploy pauses the dispatch consumer. Points keep arriving. The rider still sees the car move. Write what dispatch is allowed to do with a point whose event time is 10 seconds ago, and how a restart catches up without assigning from the backlog as if it were now.","reviewIntro":"Compare this with your journal. The age bound is the whole deep dive.","review":["Sync path for the phone: ack the append quickly. Async path: update the latest-point store and the geo index. Dispatch query: filter by age, then cell. Rider push: coalesce to the latest.","Catch-up skips to latest for dispatch. Replaying the backlog into the matcher would assign drivers to where they were, not where they are. Chapter 5's lag is acceptable for the map and not for the match."],"rubric":[["Scope","One stream, two consumers with different freshness"],["Estimates","100,000 points/s and a 2 second bound"],["Source of truth","The log. Matcher state is derived."],["Diagram","Ingest sync. Dispatch and rider async and separate."],["Deep dive","Lagging dispatch must not use stale points"],["Failure","Restart jumps to latest for matching"],["Tradeoff","Dropped map points, so the socket survives"]]},{"file":"week-07-uber-dispatch","weekLabel":"Week 7 · Marketplace","title":"Dispatch and the fare auth","minutes":"90 min","company":"Google / Microsoft, fare path like Visa","timed":false,"brief":"Match a rider to a driver, and place one fare authorization for that trip. Uber is the case. The interview bar is Google or Microsoft for the match, and Visa for the charge that follows it.","goal":"","idea":"The assignment is a compare-and-set on the driver, not a read followed by a hopeful write. The geo index makes the candidate lookup cheap. The fare auth uses the trip id as its idempotency key.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapter 6 — Partition the lookup, and chapter 7 — the assignment transaction","ddiaBody":"Chapter 6: do not scan every driver. Address them so nearby is a small set of partitions or cells. Chapter 7: the driver's status flip and the trip row commit together, or you will assign one driver twice.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Google or Microsoft, plus Visa on the fare","barBody":"Google wants the index, the match latency, and the double-assign race. Visa wants one auth per trip id, even if the match service retries. A 30 second location cache is not a dispatch input.","caseBody":"Uber's H3 post describes a hexagonal index for marketplace analysis, including pricing and dispatch. A design inspired by that index searches nearby cells instead of every driver, then uses a concurrency-safe assignment. Those search and assignment steps are this exercise. The post does not document them as Uber's production dispatch algorithm. The fare key is also this exercise.","caseUrl":"https://www.uber.com/blog/h3/","caseLink":"Uber Engineering — H3","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"2 s","label":"Max location age"},{"value":"300 ms","label":"p99 to return a match or a queued state"},{"value":"1","label":"Drivers assigned per trip"},{"value":"1","label":"Fare auths per trip id"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Lookup","Neighbor cells, not a full table scan","Match latency"],["Assignment","Compare-and-set on driver status","Double assign"],["Fare","Idempotency key equals trip id","Double auth"],["Location","Age bound around 2 seconds","Stale dispatch"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"index","prompt":"How do you find candidate drivers?","sound":"cells","staff":"A geospatial index, search the rider's cell and a ring of neighbors, filter by age and status. A SQL scan of distance for every free driver will not meet 300 ms in a big city. Say the edge: a driver just across a cell boundary.","options":[{"value":"cells","label":"Cell index plus neighbor cells, with an age filter."},{"value":"scan","label":"Compute distance to every free driver."},{"value":"cache","label":"A 30 second cache of the nearest driver per neighborhood."}]},{"id":"assign","prompt":"How do you make the assignment exclusive?","sound":"cas","staff":"Conditional update: driver is free, set to assigned, trip id recorded, one transaction. Two matchers can both read free. Only one update may succeed. Chapter 7 is this conditional update.","options":[{"value":"cas","label":"Conditional update on driver status in one transaction."},{"value":"read-write","label":"Read free drivers, then insert the trip."},{"value":"client","label":"The rider app picks a driver and tells them."}]},{"id":"fare","prompt":"What is the fare auth's idempotency key?","sound":"trip","staff":"The trip id. A retry of the match must not place a second hold. Visa's question is this key. If the match fails after the auth, the compensating action is a void of that same auth, not a second charge.","options":[{"value":"trip","label":"Trip id. A retry replays the same auth."},{"value":"attempt","label":"A new key every time the matcher runs."},{"value":"rider-day","label":"One auth per rider per day."}]}],"failureTitle":"Two riders match to one driver, and one trip auths twice","failureBody":"Two match requests read the same driver as free. Both insert a trip. The fare call for the first trip times out and the matcher calls it again with a new key. Write the conditional update and the fare key that make both bugs boring.","reviewIntro":"Index for lookup. Transaction for assignment. Trip id for money.","review":["Sync path: cell lookup, conditional assign, fare auth under the trip id, return the driver or a queued state inside 300 ms. Async path: location updates into the index, receipts, and the void if the rider cancels before pickup.","Location updates are a stream. The assignment is a transaction. Do not mix those consistencies. A driver location 30 seconds old is filtered out before the conditional update. Google grades the race. Visa grades the key."],"rubric":[["Scope","Match plus one fare auth, not the whole app"],["Estimates","300 ms and a 2 second location age"],["Source of truth","Driver status row and the trip row. The index is derived."],["Diagram","Sync assign and auth. Async location stream."],["Deep dive","The compare-and-set"],["Failure","One driver, one trip. One trip, one auth."],["Tradeoff","A queued state instead of a slow perfect search"]]},{"file":"week-08-slo-and-metrics","weekLabel":"Week 8 · Percentiles","title":"SLOs for a payment and a model","minutes":"90 min","company":"Google / Capital One","timed":false,"brief":"Write the SLOs for a card authorization path and for the fraud model that sits beside it. An average will not pass this interview.","goal":"","idea":"Tail latency is the user experience. A success-rate SLO that counts a double charge as two successes is a bad SLO. Correctness burns a different budget than latency.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapter 1 — Percentiles, and operability","ddiaBody":"Chapter 1 asks for percentiles because averages hide the tail. Use p99 for the auth deadline and for the model deadline. Then add a correctness indicator that an average latency graph cannot see.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Google SRE habits and Capital One","barBody":"Google wants the percentile, the window, and what you stop shipping when the budget is gone. Capital One wants a separate signal for double charges, wrong declines, and model-down fallback. One green graph is not both.","caseBody":"The Simian Army work only matters if you can see the failure in an SLO. Tie the indicator to a user symptom: auth p99, and a count of duplicate holds. A model p50 can look healthy while the tail blows the card deadline.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/the-netflix-simian-army-16e57fbab116","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — The Netflix Simian Army","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"p99","label":"Latency SLO, not the average"},{"value":"200 ms","label":"Auth decision budget"},{"value":"40 ms","label":"Model budget inside that"},{"value":"0","label":"Duplicate holds inside the SLO"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Auth latency","p99 under 200 ms over 5 minutes","Fleet and timeouts"],["Model latency","p99 under 40 ms, else fallback","Does not consume the whole auth budget"],["Duplicate holds","Counted, paged, not an error budget you spend","Correctness"],["Decline rate","A metric, not automatically a failure","Declines can be correct"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"latency","prompt":"Which latency number is the SLO?","sound":"p99","staff":"p99 over a short window, for auth and for the model separately. The average can sit at 30 ms while the tail is 2 seconds and customers double-tap. Chapter 1.","options":[{"value":"p99","label":"p99 for auth and p99 for the model."},{"value":"avg","label":"Average latency. Easier to explain."},{"value":"p50","label":"Median. Typical customer."}]},{"id":"success","prompt":"What does a payment success SLO include?","sound":"split","staff":"Split it. Latency and availability are one pair. Duplicate holds, lost voids, and ledger imbalance are correctness and do not spend the same error budget. A decline can be a successful decision. Capital One will push on this.","options":[{"value":"split","label":"Latency SLO separate from a correctness count."},{"value":"approved","label":"Percent of charges that were approved. Higher is better."},{"value":"http","label":"HTTP 200 rate. 200 means healthy."}]},{"id":"budget","prompt":"The latency budget is burned. A double-charge bug is also open. What ships?","sound":"stop-correctness","staff":"The double charge stops the line regardless of latency budget remaining. Latency burn stops feature work on that path. You do not spend correctness to keep a deploy calendar. Google will accept a clear policy. A bank will require it.","options":[{"value":"stop-correctness","label":"Correctness bugs stop the line. Latency burn stops features."},{"value":"ship","label":"Ship anyway. The quarter needs the feature."},{"value":"latency-only","label":"Only latency can stop a ship. Money bugs are backlog."}]}],"failureTitle":"Average auth latency is 40 ms. Customers are double-tapping.","failureBody":"The dashboard average looks calm. p99 is 2 seconds, so the terminal retries, and some retries used a new idempotency key before last week's fix. The model p50 is fine and p99 is 900 ms, so it is eating the auth budget. Name the three indicators you would page on.","reviewIntro":"Three lines on one dashboard: auth p99, model p99, duplicate-hold count.","review":["Page on auth p99, model p99 or model-timeout rate, and any duplicate hold. Do not page on decline rate alone. Declines are a product outcome. Plot them, investigate shifts, and do not call a legitimate decline an error.","Fallback when the model misses 40 ms: a rules decision you have named, still inside 200 ms, still one idempotency key. The SLO is how you notice the tail before the retry storm. Chapter 1's percentile is an operations tool, not a vocabulary word."],"rubric":[["Scope","Two SLOs and one correctness counter"],["Estimates","200 ms and 40 ms, as percentiles"],["Source of truth","Ledger for duplicates. Metrics are derived."],["Diagram","Where the timers sit on the sync path"],["Deep dive","Why average hid the double-tap"],["Failure","Retry still uses one key while you fix the tail"],["Tradeoff","Pages that wake humans only for tail and correctness"]]},{"file":"week-08-video-delivery","weekLabel":"Week 8 · Derived bytes","title":"Video path and entitlement","minutes":"90 min","company":"Google / Microsoft, entitlement like a bank","timed":false,"brief":"Design upload, transcode, and playback for a streaming title. You are not designing a CDN protocol. You are deciding what is source, what is derived, and why a failed payment must stop playback.","goal":"","idea":"Masters and renditions live in durable object storage. Transcode is a batch job with an idempotency key. The CDN is a cache. Entitlement is a ledger-shaped fact and is not cached for a day.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 10 and 12 — Batch jobs and derived artifacts","ddiaBody":"Chapter 10 is the transcode job: offline, retryable, deterministic given the master and the settings. Chapter 12 is the rendition and the CDN copy: both rebuildable. Entitlement is not derived from the CDN.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Google or Microsoft, with a payments constraint","barBody":"Google wants the async transcode and the cache. A bank-shaped reviewer wants the entitlement check on the playback path to follow the payment state, including a chargeback, without waiting for a CDN object to expire.","caseBody":"Read the Open Connect overview for the decision to put bytes close to viewers and to treat the CDN as a delivery tier, not as the archive. Write what they gave up: operational complexity at the edge, in exchange for not hauling every play from a single origin.","caseUrl":"https://openconnect.netflix.com/Open-Connect-Overview.pdf","caseLink":"Netflix Open Connect overview","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"Hours","label":"Transcode lag you can defend"},{"value":"Seconds","label":"Playback start after entitlement"},{"value":"1","label":"Master object, the source"},{"value":"0","label":"Days a revoked entitlement may keep playing"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Upload","Master to object storage, then ack","Sync path is the put"],["Transcode","Async job, key is master id plus settings","Retries"],["Playback bytes","CDN cache of renditions","Scale"],["Entitlement","Payment state, checked at start","Chargeback"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"transcode","prompt":"When does transcode run?","sound":"async","staff":"After the master is durable, as a job. Doing it inside the upload request ties a multi-minute CPU job to a browser. The job key is master id plus rendition settings, so a retry does not create a conflicting output.","options":[{"value":"async","label":"Async job after a durable master put."},{"value":"sync","label":"Transcode inside the upload HTTP call."},{"value":"cdn","label":"Let the CDN transcode on first play."}]},{"id":"truth","prompt":"What is the source of truth for the bytes?","sound":"object","staff":"Object storage of the master and the renditions. The CDN is a cache with a TTL or a purge. If the CDN and the origin disagree, origin wins and the edge object can be dropped. Chapter 12.","options":[{"value":"object","label":"Object storage. CDN is a cache."},{"value":"cdn","label":"The CDN. Origin is only for uploads."},{"value":"laptop","label":"The editor's export machine until someone copies it."}]},{"id":"entitlement","prompt":"Where is 'this account may play this title' checked?","sound":"start","staff":"At playback start, against the entitlement store that follows the payment, not against a day-long CDN rule. A chargeback or a failed renewal must stop the next session. Caching the bytes is fine. Caching a yes for a day is not.","options":[{"value":"start","label":"Check entitlement at playback start. Cache the video, not the yes."},{"value":"cdn-day","label":"Bake entitlement into the CDN for 24 hours."},{"value":"never","label":"Anyone with the URL may play."}]}],"failureTitle":"A chargeback lands. The CDN still has the title.","failureBody":"The payment is reversed. Edge caches around the world still hold the rendition. A session is about to start. Write what you check, what you are allowed to leave cached, and how a retried transcode job stays tied to one master.","reviewIntro":"Bytes are derived and cacheable. Permission is not a cache key you forget about.","review":["Sync path: put master, record entitlement from the payment id. Async path: transcode job, publish renditions, CDN fill on miss. Playback: check entitlement, issue a short-lived URL, serve bytes from the edge.","Purge is for compliance speed, not for correctness. Correctness is the entitlement check at start. The transcode idempotency key prevents two workers from publishing different bytes under one rendition id. Google grades the pipeline. A payments reviewer grades the check."],"rubric":[["Scope","Upload, transcode, play, entitlement. Not CDN internals."],["Estimates","Transcode lag versus playback start"],["Source of truth","Object storage for bytes. Payment record for permission."],["Diagram","Sync put and entitlement check. Async transcode."],["Deep dive","Chargeback versus a warm CDN"],["Failure","One job key per master plus settings"],["Tradeoff","Edge cache for bytes. Fresh check for permission."]]},{"file":"week-09-double-entry-ledger","weekLabel":"Week 9 · Ledger","title":"Double-entry ledger","minutes":"90 min","company":"Monzo / Revolut / JPMorgan Chase","timed":false,"brief":"Design the ledger that every later payments workshop will assume. Postings are the facts. Balances are derived. Then run the code lab and reconcile your story with the printout.","goal":"","idea":"Every entry sums to zero. The invariant lives in the transaction, with a per-account lock or a conditional update. A balance column updated by read-then-write loses concurrent debits. Audit is the journal, not a screenshot of the balance.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 7 and 12 — Transactions and derived balances","ddiaBody":"Chapter 7: put the invariant inside the transaction. Chapter 12: a balance is derived and must be rebuildable by replaying postings. The code lab shows both, including the lost update.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Monzo, Revolut, and JPMorgan Chase","barBody":"They want balanced entries, no silent overwrite, idempotent posting, and a story for two debits racing. JPMorgan Chase will ask what an auditor replays. A QPS boast does not substitute for the invariant.","caseBody":"Michelangelo is not a ledger, and that is the lesson. Uber kept model outputs as derived signals beside operational data. Your balance is derived from postings the same way a score is derived from features: useful, rebuildable, and not allowed to drift from the source.","caseUrl":"https://www.uber.com/blog/michelangelo-machine-learning-platform/","caseLink":"Uber Engineering — Michelangelo","codeLab":"From the repo root, run python3 labs/ledger/ledger.py. You should see a trial balance of zero, a rejected unbalanced entry, a replay that does not move money, and a lost update that promises two merchants.","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"0","label":"Sum of a valid entry"},{"value":"1","label":"Partition for an account balance"},{"value":"7 years","label":"Posting retention"},{"value":"2","label":"Racing debits in the lab"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Entry","Debits and credits sum to zero","Invariant"],["Balance","Sum of postings, cache optional","Derived"],["Race","Lock the account or conditional update","Lost update"],["Replay","Idempotency key on the entry","At-least-once callers"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"balance","prompt":"Where does the available balance come from?","sound":"derived","staff":"From postings. A cached balance is allowed if a rebuild matches it and if authorization does not trust a stale cache. The lab's lost update is what happens when the column is the source.","options":[{"value":"derived","label":"Sum of postings. Cache is optional and checked."},{"value":"column","label":"A balance column updated in the application."},{"value":"sheet","label":"A nightly spreadsheet the app reads."}]},{"id":"shape","prompt":"What does one entry look like?","sound":"balanced","staff":"At least two postings that sum to zero. A single signed row with no counterparty cannot be audited. Monzo and Revolut both expect you to say double-entry without turning the interview into an accounting lecture.","options":[{"value":"balanced","label":"Two or more postings, sum zero."},{"value":"single","label":"One row: account, delta."},{"value":"overwrite","label":"Set the balance to the new number."}]},{"id":"race","prompt":"Two debits read the same balance. What serializes them?","sound":"lock","staff":"A per-account lock or a conditional update inside the posting transaction. Read-committed plus a check in the app loses the update. Chapter 7. The lab prints the bad interleaving.","options":[{"value":"lock","label":"Lock or conditional update in the posting transaction."},{"value":"app","label":"The API checks the balance, then writes, no lock."},{"value":"queue","label":"A single-threaded process for the whole bank, forever."}]}],"failureTitle":"Two 80 dollar spends both read a 100 dollar balance","failureBody":"Both requests pass the application check. Both write. Two merchants are told they will be paid. Walk the locked version and the lost-update version. Say what the trial balance is in the locked version.","reviewIntro":"The code lab is the staff review's exhibit. Match your words to it.","review":["Sync path: begin transaction, lock the account, insert balanced postings with the idempotency key, commit, return the derived balance. Async path: feed projection, statements, fraud features. Those are rebuildable.","An auditor replays postings. JPMorgan Chase will ask for immutability: corrections are new entries, not edits. Revolut will ask for the available-versus-ledger balance distinction if you bring it up. Do not bring it up unless you can define both."],"rubric":[["Scope","Posting and balance, not the card network"],["Estimates","Retention and the single-account lock"],["Source of truth","Postings. Balance derived."],["Diagram","Sync post. Async projections."],["Deep dive","The race on one account"],["Failure","Second debit declines or waits. Replay does not repost."],["Tradeoff","Lock contention on hot accounts, accepted to keep the invariant"]]},{"file":"week-09-payment-state-machine","weekLabel":"Week 9 · State machine","title":"Auth, capture, refund","minutes":"90 min","company":"Visa / Capital One / Revolut","timed":false,"brief":"Model a card payment as explicit states. Illegal edges fail. History is append-only. A retry of capture must not capture twice.","goal":"","idea":"The state machine is the invariant. Each transition has an idempotency key and a legal source state. Refunds and voids are new postings, not edits of the capture row.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapter 7 — Multi-row transitions","ddiaBody":"Chapter 7 covers a change that must update the payment state and the ledger posting together. A crash between them is an unknown outcome you reconcile, not a license to repeat the side effect.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Visa, Capital One, and Revolut","barBody":"Visa lives in auth versus capture versus refund. Capital One wants the audit of who moved the state. Revolut wants the ledger posting on capture, not on a vague 'paid' flag. Partial capture cannot exceed the remaining auth.","caseBody":"Use the outbox lesson from your own lab as the case. A state transition and its ledger posting commit together, and anything downstream is an outbox row. The Simian Army stance still applies: the process can die between two writes if you split them.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/the-netflix-simian-army-16e57fbab116","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — The Netflix Simian Army","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"4","label":"States you must be able to say"},{"value":"1","label":"Captures applied per capture key"},{"value":"≤ auth","label":"Partial capture versus remaining auth"},{"value":"Append","label":"How a refund is recorded"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Auth","Hold, no merchant settlement yet","State authorized"],["Capture","Posting, up to remaining auth","State captured"],["Void","Release remaining hold","State voided"],["Refund","New posting after capture","Append-only"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"states","prompt":"How do you represent the payment?","sound":"machine","staff":"Explicit states and a table of legal edges: authorized, captured, voided, refunded, and partial versions if you can define them. A boolean 'paid' cannot express a void after auth or a refund after capture. Visa will ask for the edges.","options":[{"value":"machine","label":"Named states. Illegal edges rejected."},{"value":"flag","label":"A paid boolean."},{"value":"text","label":"A free-text status the client sets."}]},{"id":"capture","prompt":"What may a capture do?","sound":"bounded","staff":"Capture up to the remaining authorized amount, once per capture key. A retry returns the stored capture. Capturing above the auth, or applying the same key twice, is the defect.","options":[{"value":"bounded","label":"Amount at most the remaining auth. Same key replays."},{"value":"full-always","label":"Always capture the original amount again."},{"value":"any","label":"Any amount. The merchant knows best."}]},{"id":"refund","prompt":"How is a refund stored?","sound":"append","staff":"A new posting and a new transition, linked to the capture. Editing the capture amount destroys the audit. JPMorgan Chase and Capital One both want the original and the reversal visible.","options":[{"value":"append","label":"Append a refund posting. Do not edit the capture."},{"value":"edit","label":"Reduce the capture amount in place."},{"value":"delete","label":"Delete the payment and insert a smaller one."}]}],"failureTitle":"Capture retry and a refund race","failureBody":"The first capture committed and the response was lost. The merchant retries. At the same time a refund is submitted for that payment. Write the legal edges and which key makes the capture retry a replay rather than a second posting.","reviewIntro":"Draw the edges. Put a key on each edge. Append the money.","review":["Sync path: load payment, reject illegal edges, write the new state and the ledger posting in one transaction with the transition key, outbox the rest. Async path: settlement file, notifications.","If capture and refund truly race, the transaction serializes them. One commits. The other sees the new state and follows the edge from there, or fails cleanly. Revolut wants no negative merchant position from a double capture. Visa wants the auth amount to bound the capture."],"rubric":[["Scope","Auth, capture, void, refund"],["Estimates","You can skip hero QPS. The edges matter more."],["Source of truth","Payment state plus postings, one transaction"],["Diagram","Sync transition. Async settlement."],["Deep dive","Retry of capture versus a racing refund"],["Failure","Same capture key, one posting"],["Tradeoff","More states to name, so illegal money movement is rejected"]]},{"file":"week-10-fraud-decision","weekLabel":"Week 10 · Fraud","title":"Fraud decision under a deadline","minutes":"90 min","company":"Capital One / Visa / Revolut","timed":false,"brief":"Score a card authorization with a model. The auth has a latency budget. The model will time out. The ledger still posts only on a durable decision, and a retry must not approve twice.","goal":"","idea":"The model is a signal with a deadline. Features that catch card testing are a stream. Stable risk can be batch. If the model misses the deadline, a named fallback runs. Humans review a tail asynchronously. They do not sit on the cardholder's critical path for every swipe.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 10 and 11 — Batch features, stream features, sync decision","ddiaBody":"Chapter 10 builds the slow features. Chapter 11 updates velocity in near real time. The request path joins them and must still finish inside the auth budget. The score is derived. The decision row is the fact.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Capital One, Visa, and Revolut","barBody":"Capital One asks what you do when the model is down. Visa asks whether the auth deadline still holds. Revolut asks whether a fallback can double-charge or approve a replay. Availability of the model is not availability of the ledger.","caseBody":"Read Michelangelo for the decision to own feature computation in a platform so online and training do not drift. You need that for fraud velocity. The ledger posting is still yours, and Michelangelo is not allowed to become it.","caseUrl":"https://www.uber.com/blog/michelangelo-machine-learning-platform/","caseLink":"Uber Engineering — Michelangelo","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"200 ms","label":"Auth budget"},{"value":"40 ms","label":"Model budget"},{"value":"2%","label":"Tail sent to human review"},{"value":"1","label":"Decision rows per attempt key"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Stream feature","Spend velocity over minutes","Card testing"],["Batch feature","Stable customer risk","Can be hours old"],["Fallback","Rules you can name, inside the auth budget","Model down"],["Review","Async queue for the tail, with a timeout","Not the swipe path"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"budget","prompt":"The model does not answer in time. What runs?","sound":"fallback","staff":"A named fallback inside the remaining auth budget: step-up, rules decline, or a narrow approve you can defend. Waiting 800 ms for the model misses the terminal and causes retries. Capital One wants the fallback written down, not 'we'll see.'","options":[{"value":"fallback","label":"Fallback inside the budget. Record that the model was skipped."},{"value":"wait","label":"Wait for the model. Accuracy over latency."},{"value":"approve","label":"Approve everything when the model is down, so the bank stays up."}]},{"id":"features","prompt":"Which features must be fresh?","sound":"velocity","staff":"Velocity and device signals come from a stream. Long-run risk can be batch. Scoring card testing on yesterday's counts misses the attack happening now. Chapter 11 versus chapter 10.","options":[{"value":"velocity","label":"Stream the short-window velocity. Batch the stable risk."},{"value":"batch-all","label":"Nightly features only. Simpler pipeline."},{"value":"raw","label":"Send the raw transaction history on every swipe."}]},{"id":"review","prompt":"Where do humans sit?","sound":"async","staff":"Async, on a small tail, with a hold state that has a timeout and an idempotent resolution. They do not stand in front of every auth. Their decision posts or releases through the same payment state machine, one key.","options":[{"value":"async","label":"Async tail, timeout, same state machine."},{"value":"sync","label":"Every auth waits for an analyst."},{"value":"none","label":"No humans. The model is final even when it is down."}]}],"failureTitle":"Model timeout, then the client retries the auth","failureBody":"The model misses 40 ms. Your fallback runs. The response is lost. The terminal retries. A second path approves again because the first decision was not stored under the attempt key. Write the decision row and what the human queue is allowed to do later.","reviewIntro":"Deadline, fallback, one decision row, ledger posts only from that row.","review":["Sync path: load stream and batch features, call the model with a 40 ms budget, else fallback, commit one decision row keyed by the auth attempt, then the payment transition. Async path: feature updates, analyst queue, model monitoring.","If analysts later reverse, they use the payment state machine, append-only. They do not edit the decision row's history. Revolut wants the customer-visible state to match that machine. Visa wants the terminal to have received one answer inside its deadline."],"rubric":[["Scope","Score plus fallback plus review tail"],["Estimates","40 ms inside 200 ms, and the 2 percent tail"],["Source of truth","Decision row and ledger. Score is derived."],["Diagram","Sync decision. Async review and features."],["Deep dive","Model down"],["Failure","Retry replays the decision. It does not approve again."],["Tradeoff","A simpler fallback over a late, smarter score"]]},{"file":"week-10-reconciliation","weekLabel":"Week 10 · Reconciliation","title":"Reconcile after a processor outage","minutes":"90 min","company":"Visa / JPMorgan Chase / Capital One","timed":false,"brief":"Your ledger says a set of captures failed because the processor timed out. The next morning the processor's file says many of them succeeded. Design the match, the breaks, and what you are not allowed to do to the ledger.","goal":"","idea":"Reconciliation compares two logs. It does not elect a winner and overwrite. Streaming matches catch breaks early. A batch file is the official pass. Breaks become cases. Adjustments are new postings with a reason.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 8, 10, and 12 — Unknown outcomes, batch compare, derived match","ddiaBody":"Chapter 8 created the unknown timeout. Chapter 10 is the nightly file compare. Chapter 12 says the match result is derived: you can recompute it from the two sources. You still post explicit entries to change money.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Visa, JPMorgan Chase, and Capital One","barBody":"They will fail a design that updates the ledger in place to mirror the processor file. They want a match key that is not 'amount plus roughly the same time,' a break queue, and an audit of who resolved it.","caseBody":"This is the financial version of comparing a source log with a derived view. Netflix rebuilds a homepage from source signals. You rebuild a match view from your ledger and their file. The difference: a mismatch in money becomes a case, not a silent recompute of balances.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/system-architectures-for-personalization-and-recommendation-e081aa94b5d8","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — derived views, as a contrast","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"1.2M","label":"Dollars of timeout captures in the scenario"},{"value":"Nightly","label":"Official batch reconcile"},{"value":"Minutes","label":"Streaming match lag target"},{"value":"0","label":"Silent ledger overwrites"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Match key","Your id and the processor reference","Not amount plus timestamp"],["Streaming","Match webhooks as they arrive","Early breaks"],["Batch","Official file, recomputable","End-of-day books"],["Break","A case. Adjustment is a new posting.","Audit"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"when","prompt":"When do you reconcile?","sound":"both","staff":"Both. A streaming match raises breaks within minutes. The nightly file is the official pass. Only nightly means you learn about a million dollars tomorrow. Only streaming means you have no official artifact when a webhook was the thing that lied.","options":[{"value":"both","label":"Streaming match plus a nightly official file."},{"value":"nightly","label":"Nightly file only."},{"value":"never","label":"The webhook is always right. No reconcile."}]},{"id":"key","prompt":"What joins your row to theirs?","sound":"ids","staff":"Your idempotency or payment id and their processor reference, stored at auth time. Amount is a check, not the key. Amount plus time collides and misses. JPMorgan Chase will ask this immediately.","options":[{"value":"ids","label":"Stored ids on both sides. Amount is a check."},{"value":"amount-time","label":"Amount and a timestamp within five minutes."},{"value":"name","label":"Cardholder name."}]},{"id":"break","prompt":"The file shows a capture you marked failed. What changes the ledger?","sound":"case","staff":"Open a break. A defined rule or a human resolves it by posting an explicit adjusting entry linked to both ids. Do not run an update that sets your row equal to theirs. Capital One and Visa both treat that as an unauditable write.","options":[{"value":"case","label":"Break case, then an explicit adjusting posting."},{"value":"overwrite","label":"Update the ledger to match the file."},{"value":"ignore","label":"Keep your failed mark. The file is their problem."}]}],"failureTitle":"Processor file lists captures you recorded as timeouts","failureBody":"Overnight the file contains 1.2 million dollars of captures your ledger marked failed because of timeouts. Some webhooks were duplicates. Some never arrived. Write the match, what a break looks like, and the posting that is allowed to move money afterward.","reviewIntro":"Two sources, one match view, zero silent overwrites.","review":["Store the processor reference on the way out. Stream: match webhook to that id, idempotent. Batch: replay the file into a match table keyed by those ids, classify matched, missing, and extra. Breaks have owners.","The adjusting entry references the break id, the original payment id, and the processor id. That is the audit. Recomputing the match is safe. Recomputing customer balances from the processor file is not. Chapter 8's unknown outcome ends here, in a case, not in a second charge."],"rubric":[["Scope","Match and breaks, not a new card scheme"],["Estimates","File size and how fast a break must be visible"],["Source of truth","Your ledger and their file are both inputs. The match is derived."],["Diagram","Streaming match and nightly batch"],["Deep dive","Timeout marked failed, file says captured"],["Failure","Duplicate webhooks do not create two breaks that both post"],["Tradeoff","Humans or strict rules on breaks, instead of a silent fix"]]},{"file":"week-11-payment-platform-drill","weekLabel":"Week 11 · Timed drill","title":"Payment platform slice","minutes":"45 min drill","company":"Revolut / Visa / JPMorgan Chase","timed":true,"brief":"Closed book. Forty-five minutes. A slice of a card payment platform: charge, ledger, processor webhook. The journal is the whole answer.","goal":"","idea":"You already have the pieces: one posting transaction, one idempotency key, an outbox, and reconciliation for the unknown webhook. This drill is whether you can assemble them without notes.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 7, 8, and 11 — Transaction, timeout, stream","ddiaBody":"Chapter 7 commits the charge once. Chapter 8 says the webhook's absence is not a failure. Chapter 11 carries the durable event to settlement. Name those three in the journal.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Revolut, Visa, and JPMorgan Chase","barBody":"Revolut grades the invariant. Visa grades auth versus the later webhook. JPMorgan Chase grades the audit and the refusal to overwrite the ledger from a webhook. The review stays sealed.","caseBody":"Debrief. Sync: idempotency key, balanced posting or a hold, decision row, outbox, ack. Async: webhook applied by processor event id, at-least-once, second delivery is a replay. Missing webhook: nightly file, break case, explicit adjusting entry. Do not let the webhook be the source of truth. It is an input to reconciliation and to the state machine.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/the-netflix-simian-army-16e57fbab116","caseLink":"After the drill: partial failure is normal","codeLab":"","sealed":"Interview prompt. Design a slice that accepts a card charge, posts a ledger, and accepts a processor webhook that may arrive twice or not at all. Average 150 charges per second, peak 1,500. p99 decision 200 ms. Duplicate webhooks must not post twice. A missing webhook is reconciled, not guessed. You have 45 minutes. Write the seven moves in the journal.","numbers":[{"value":"150 /s","label":"Average charges"},{"value":"1,500 /s","label":"Peak charges"},{"value":"200 ms","label":"p99 decision"},{"value":"2+","label":"Times a webhook may be delivered"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Peak","1,500 charges/s","Fleet, not storage"],["Average","150/s","Storage and reconcile volume"],["Webhook","At least once","Idempotent apply"],["Missing webhook","A break, not a guess","Nightly file"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"truth","prompt":"What is the source of truth?","sound":"ledger","staff":"Your ledger and payment state, committed with the idempotency key. The webhook updates that machine. It does not replace it. JPMorgan Chase stops the interview if the webhook is the ledger.","options":[{"value":"ledger","label":"Your ledger. The webhook is an input."},{"value":"webhook","label":"The latest webhook."},{"value":"client","label":"Whatever the mobile app displayed."}]},{"id":"webhook","prompt":"A webhook is delivered twice. What posts?","sound":"once","staff":"One apply, keyed by the processor event id, stored uniquely. The second delivery replays the stored effect. Visa expects this. A second posting is a defect even if both webhooks were 'success.'","options":[{"value":"once","label":"Unique processor event id. Second delivery replays."},{"value":"twice","label":"Apply both. The processor must mean it."},{"value":"ignore","label":"Ignore webhooks. Poll once a day only."}]},{"id":"missing","prompt":"No webhook arrives for a charge you submitted.","sound":"reconcile","staff":"Leave the state unknown or pending, and reconcile against the file or a status query using the original id. Do not mark it failed and allow a new key to charge the customer again. Chapter 8.","options":[{"value":"reconcile","label":"Pending, then reconcile on the original id."},{"value":"failed","label":"Mark failed so the user can tap pay again with a new key."},{"value":"success","label":"Mark succeeded so the UI looks calm."}]}],"failureTitle":"Webhook twice, and a different charge with no webhook","failureBody":"Event A arrives twice. Event B never arrives, and the client timed out. Write both outcomes, the keys, and what the ledger contains at the end.","reviewIntro":"Grade your journal against this, move by move. Missing the unknown-outcome case is the usual fail.","review":["Charge path commits at most one posting per client key. Webhook path commits at most one transition per processor event id. Reconciliation posts an explicit adjustment per break id.","Peak 1,500/s is a modest fleet if the transaction is a single-account posting. Do not spend the 45 minutes on a new database. Spend it on the two keys and the pending state. That is the Revolut, Visa, and JPMorgan Chase overlap."],"rubric":[["Scope","Charge, ledger, webhook. Not disputes, unless you had time."],["Estimates","Peak for latency, average for the file"],["Source of truth","Ledger, not the webhook"],["Diagram","Sync charge. Async webhook and nightly file."],["Deep dive","Duplicate webhook or the missing one. Pick one."],["Failure","Two keys: client attempt, processor event"],["Tradeoff","Pending state instead of a confident wrong status"]]},{"file":"week-11-wallet-payouts","weekLabel":"Week 11 · Invariants","title":"Wallet and payouts","minutes":"90 min","company":"Revolut / Monzo","timed":false,"brief":"Design a multi-currency wallet and a payout to a bank. Three invariants: no negative available balance, no lost or duplicated payout, and an audit trail. FX is a balanced pair of postings, not a magic number.","goal":"","idea":"The available balance check sits inside the posting transaction. A payout is a pending instruction with an idempotency key, then a settlement when the bank confirms. Marking it paid before the bank call is how you pay twice after a timeout.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 7 and 8 — Invariants and unknown bank calls","ddiaBody":"Chapter 7 is the no-negative-balance check inside the transaction. Chapter 8 is the bank call that times out after the money may already have moved. The pending state is how you survive chapter 8 without breaking chapter 7.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Revolut and Monzo","barBody":"They will spend the interview on invariants, not on QPS. Multi-currency means separate accounts in the ledger plus an FX entry that sums to zero in a stated pair. A single blended balance number is how FX bugs hide.","caseBody":"The outbox lab is the case. The payout instruction is an outbox row committed with the hold on available funds. The bank call happens after. Reconciliation later matches the bank statement to that instruction id.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/the-netflix-simian-army-16e57fbab116","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — practice the timeout","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"0","label":"Negative available balances allowed"},{"value":"1","label":"Bank wires per payout id"},{"value":"2","label":"Postings in an FX conversion"},{"value":"Pending","label":"State before the bank confirms"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Available funds","Checked inside the posting transaction","No overdraft"],["Payout","Pending, idempotent bank instruction, then settled","No double wire"],["FX","Per-currency accounts, one balanced conversion entry","No blended number"],["Audit","Postings plus the instruction id","Regulator"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"overdraft","prompt":"Where is 'no negative available balance' enforced?","sound":"transaction","staff":"In the posting transaction, under the account lock. An application check before the call is the lost-update bug from the ledger lab. Monzo will ask you to put the check next to the insert.","options":[{"value":"transaction","label":"Inside the posting transaction."},{"value":"app","label":"In the API, then insert with no lock."},{"value":"nightly","label":"A nightly job fixes negatives."}]},{"id":"payout","prompt":"When do you mark a payout paid?","sound":"confirmed","staff":"When the bank confirms, or when reconciliation matches the statement. Before that it is pending, and the funds are held. Marking paid and then calling the bank will retry into a second wire. Same instruction id on every retry.","options":[{"value":"confirmed","label":"Pending until the bank or the statement confirms. One instruction id."},{"value":"before","label":"Mark paid, then call the bank."},{"value":"customer","label":"Mark paid when the user taps the button."}]},{"id":"fx","prompt":"How do you record a currency conversion?","sound":"pair","staff":"Two postings that balance: decrease one currency account, increase the other, with the rate and the external quote id on the entry. A single number that changes denomination cannot be audited. Revolut will ask where the rate lives.","options":[{"value":"pair","label":"A balanced pair of postings, rate stored on the entry."},{"value":"blend","label":"One balance field, multiplied by a rate in the app."},{"value":"skip","label":"Disallow FX. Out of scope, said explicitly, is acceptable if the prompt allows it."}]}],"failureTitle":"The bank call times out after the wire may have been sent","failureBody":"You held the funds and called the bank. The client timed out. You do not know if the wire exists. The user taps payout again. Write the states, the id, and what must be true of the available balance the whole time.","reviewIntro":"Hold, one instruction, confirm, then settle. FX balances. Negatives never commit.","review":["Sync path: lock account, check available, post the hold, insert payout row and outbox with one id, commit. Async path: call the bank with that id, reconcile, post settlement or release. A retry of the user tap returns the same payout.","Available balance stays non-negative because the hold committed before the call. A second tap does not create a second hold. If the bank never acknowledges, an operator resolves a break, the same way as processor reconciliation. That is the regulatory story Revolut and Monzo expect."],"rubric":[["Scope","Wallet hold, payout, FX entry"],["Estimates","You may spend less time on QPS than on the states"],["Source of truth","Postings and the payout row"],["Diagram","Sync hold. Async bank call."],["Deep dive","Timeout after the wire might exist"],["Failure","One instruction id. Available balance never negative."],["Tradeoff","Funds locked while pending, so you do not pay twice"]]},{"file":"week-12-feature-platform","weekLabel":"Week 12 · Features","title":"Feature platform and train/serve skew","minutes":"90 min","company":"Google / Capital One","timed":false,"brief":"Design the feature path for a fraud model. The same definition must train and serve. A notebook that computes 'last 10 minutes' differently online is a silent model failure.","goal":"","idea":"Features are derived data with a version and a time. Training uses point-in-time values, not whatever the row says now. Online serving uses the same definition. Skew is a production incident, measured, not a research surprise.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 10 and 11 — Batch backfill and stream features","ddiaBody":"Chapter 10 is the backfill that rebuilds a feature version. Chapter 11 is the online update. Chapter 12's rule applies: if you cannot recompute the feature from source events, you cannot audit a decision.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Google and Capital One","barBody":"Google wants the offline store, the online store, and the shared definition. Capital One wants point-in-time correctness for a decision you may have to explain later, and a way to see skew before the loss rate moves.","caseBody":"Read Michelangelo for the decision to put feature computation in a platform instead of in each team's notebook. Write what that costs: teams move slower at first, and they stop shipping unreproducible scores. That is the tradeoff.","caseUrl":"https://www.uber.com/blog/michelangelo-machine-learning-platform/","caseLink":"Uber Engineering — Michelangelo","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"1 def","label":"Definition shared by train and serve"},{"value":"Point-in-time","label":"Training join"},{"value":"Minutes","label":"Freshness for velocity features"},{"value":"Versioned","label":"Backfills"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Source","Immutable events","Recompute"],["Offline store","Point-in-time snapshots for training","Audit"],["Online store","Latest values for the scorer, same definition","The 40 ms read"],["Skew metric","Online versus offline on the same keys","Silent failure"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"definition","prompt":"Who owns the feature definition?","sound":"platform","staff":"One definition, executed in batch and online. A notebook that reimplements the window will drift. Michelangelo's point. Capital One cannot explain a decline from a feature nobody can recompute.","options":[{"value":"platform","label":"One versioned definition, two execution modes."},{"value":"notebook","label":"Each scientist writes the training SQL and the serving code separately."},{"value":"model","label":"The model file embeds a copy of the features."}]},{"id":"time","prompt":"How do you build a training row?","sound":"pit","staff":"Point-in-time: features as they were known at decision time, not as they look after later events. Training on the future is leakage. Google will ask this if you say 'we join the feature table.'","options":[{"value":"pit","label":"Point-in-time join against versioned features."},{"value":"latest","label":"Join to whatever the feature is today."},{"value":"random","label":"Sample current customers and use their current features."}]},{"id":"backfill","prompt":"How do you correct a bad feature?","sound":"version","staff":"A versioned backfill from source events, then a new training set. An ad hoc update of online keys with no version leaves training and serving permanently different. Chapter 10 is the backfill.","options":[{"value":"version","label":"Versioned backfill from events."},{"value":"patch","label":"Update the online value in place. Skip training."},{"value":"ignore","label":"Leave it. The model will average it out."}]}],"failureTitle":"Online 'last 10 minutes' does not match training","failureBody":"Training counted settled transactions in a tumbling window. Online counts attempts in a sliding window, including declines. Fraud loss moves and the offline metric still looks fine. Write how you would have detected this, and what you recompute.","reviewIntro":"One definition, point-in-time training, a skew chart.","review":["Events are source. Offline and online stores are derived. The scorer reads online by entity id, one partition, inside the fraud budget from week 10. Training jobs read snapshots. A skew job compares them on a sample of decision ids.","The ledger is still the source of truth for money. Features must not be read back as balances. Capital One's audit is: given a decision id, show the feature version and the values. If you cannot, the platform is a notebook with extra steps."],"rubric":[["Scope","Feature platform for one fraud model, not all of ML"],["Estimates","Online read inside the model budget"],["Source of truth","Events. Stores are derived. Ledger is separate."],["Diagram","Batch backfill and stream update, one definition"],["Deep dive","The skew you would graph"],["Failure","A bad version is replaced by a recompute, not a silent edit"],["Tradeoff","Slower feature changes, reproducible decisions"]]},{"file":"week-12-model-serving","weekLabel":"Week 12 · Serving","title":"Model serving and fallback","minutes":"90 min","company":"Google / Capital One","timed":false,"brief":"Serve the fraud model beside authorization. Batching can help a GPU. It cannot spend the cardholder's budget. If the model is down, the ledger still posts only from a durable decision, using the fallback you already named.","goal":"","idea":"Tail latency and retry amplification are the serving design. A queue of GPU work that ignores the deadline will melt under client retries. Shed, fallback, and keep one idempotency key.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 1 and 8 — Tail latency and overload","ddiaBody":"Chapter 1: p99, not the mean batching efficiency. Chapter 8: a timed-out score is an unknown, and the client's retry is extra load. Bound in-flight work.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Google and Capital One","barBody":"Google wants batching, timeouts, and load shedding. Capital One wants the fallback and a guarantee that a slow model cannot double-post a charge. The score is not a posting.","caseBody":"Michelangelo's serving lesson is that models are versioned artifacts behind a stable feature fetch. Use that. Then put a deadline in front, which a platform blog will under-emphasize and an auth path cannot.","caseUrl":"https://www.uber.com/blog/michelangelo-machine-learning-platform/","caseLink":"Uber Engineering — Michelangelo","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"40 ms","label":"Score deadline"},{"value":"p99","label":"The latency you operate"},{"value":"1","label":"Decision rows per attempt"},{"value":"Bounded","label":"In-flight GPU work"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Deadline","40 ms, then fallback","Auth budget stays intact"],["Batching","Only inside the deadline, or on an async path","GPU efficiency"],["Overload","Shed. Do not queue without bound.","Retry storm"],["Posting","From the decision row, never from the score","Ledger"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"batch","prompt":"Where is batching allowed?","sound":"inside","staff":"Inside the deadline, a few milliseconds of wait to fill a batch, or on an async path that is not the swipe. A batcher that waits for a full batch will blow p99. Google wants this sentence.","options":[{"value":"inside","label":"Tiny wait inside the deadline, or batch only off the swipe path."},{"value":"full","label":"Always wait for a full GPU batch."},{"value":"never","label":"One request, one model call, no timeout story."}]},{"id":"down","prompt":"GPUs are unhealthy. What does auth do?","sound":"rules","staff":"Rules fallback, decision row records model-down, ledger posts from that decision only. Approving all is a fraud incident. Declining all is an outage you might choose on purpose, and you must say so. Capital One expects the choice in advance.","options":[{"value":"rules","label":"Named rules fallback, one decision row."},{"value":"approve-all","label":"Approve all so checkout never stops."},{"value":"hang","label":"Hold the auth until a GPU returns."}]},{"id":"retry","prompt":"Clients retry because p99 spiked. What protects the GPU?","sound":"shed","staff":"A bound on in-flight scores, shed past it, and the same attempt key so retries do not create decisions. Without the key, shedding is not enough: a retry that does get through can approve again. Chapter 8 plus the ledger.","options":[{"value":"shed","label":"Shed past a bound. Same attempt key."},{"value":"queue","label":"Unbounded queue. Fairness."},{"value":"scale","label":"Assume autoscaling saves you. No shed."}]}],"failureTitle":"GPU queue builds, clients retry, load multiplies","failureBody":"p99 goes from 40 ms to 3 seconds. Terminals retry. Each retry is a new score request, and some are new idempotency keys from an old app build. Write the shed rule, the fallback, and what the ledger is allowed to post.","reviewIntro":"Deadline, shed, fallback, one decision. The GPU is replaceable. The posting is not.","review":["Sync path: feature fetch, score or fallback inside 40 ms, commit decision, payment transition. Async path: batch rescoring, drift monitors, GPU autoscaling that is not on the critical path.","A score never posts. A decision posts once. Old app builds that mint a new key are a client defect you should name, with server-side duplicate detection on account plus amount plus a short window as a backstop, said carefully so you do not block legitimate repeat purchases. Say the backstop's limit."],"rubric":[["Scope","Serving and fallback, assuming the feature store exists"],["Estimates","40 ms p99 and an in-flight bound"],["Source of truth","Decision row. Score is derived."],["Diagram","Sync score-or-fallback. Async capacity."],["Deep dive","Retry amplification"],["Failure","Shed plus one key. Ledger posts once."],["Tradeoff","Lower GPU utilization, so the tail stays inside the auth"]]},{"file":"week-13-eval-and-cost","weekLabel":"Week 13 · Eval","title":"Eval, cache, and model cost","minutes":"90 min","company":"Google / Microsoft / JPMorgan Chase","timed":false,"brief":"You are about to let advisors use the document assistant. Decide what you measure, how you route small and large models, and how a cache can avoid serving yesterday's fee schedule.","goal":"","idea":"An eval set is the SLO for a model. Cost is a capacity plan. A cache key that omits the policy version is a consistency bug: the answer is stale derived data, served confidently.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 1 and 12 — Capacity, and freshness of derived answers","ddiaBody":"Chapter 1: cost and load are design inputs. Put a number on tokens. Chapter 12: a cached answer is derived from a policy version. When the policy changes, the cache key must change or the entry must die.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Google, Microsoft, and JPMorgan Chase","barBody":"Google and Microsoft want the eval gate, the routing, and the cost envelope. JPMorgan Chase wants the cache to stop serving a superseded fee schedule, and wants citation accuracy on the eval, not a vibe check.","caseBody":"Netflix measures a ranker offline before it meets a member, and still watches online behavior. Steal the gate: a fixed eval set, including citation correctness and refusal, before an advisor sees the assistant. Online feedback does not replace that set.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/system-architectures-for-personalization-and-recommendation-e081aa94b5d8","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — offline and online evaluation, as a parallel","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"Fixed set","label":"Eval before ship"},{"value":"Version","label":"In the cache key"},{"value":"Small first","label":"Default model route"},{"value":"Budget","label":"Tokens per desk per day"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Eval","Citations correct, refusals correct, no cross-tenant","Ship gate"],["Route","Small model by default, large on low confidence","Cost"],["Cache","Key includes policy version and ACL scope","Freshness"],["Budget","A number you can exhaust","Capacity"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"gate","prompt":"What allows a ship?","sound":"eval","staff":"A fixed eval set with thresholds for citation accuracy, refusal, and tenant isolation. A hallway demo is not a gate. Microsoft and Google both expect a number. JPMorgan Chase expects the isolation case to be in the set.","options":[{"value":"eval","label":"Fixed eval, thresholds, including refusal and isolation."},{"value":"vibe","label":"Advisors try it for a day and say it feels good."},{"value":"offline-loss","label":"Only the language-model loss on training data."}]},{"id":"route","prompt":"Which model runs?","sound":"cascade","staff":"Small model by default. Escalate when retrieval confidence is low or the question is in a class you marked expensive. Always calling the largest model is a cost incident waiting for a busy Monday. Put the token budget next to the route.","options":[{"value":"cascade","label":"Small by default, escalate, with a token budget."},{"value":"large","label":"Always the largest model. Quality first."},{"value":"random","label":"Pick at random to A/B forever, with no gate."}]},{"id":"cache","prompt":"What is in the answer cache key?","sound":"versioned","staff":"Policy version, document set version, and the ACL scope. A 30-day cache of the answer text will serve a fee schedule that changed yesterday. That is a consistency bug, chapter 12, not a caching win.","options":[{"value":"versioned","label":"Version plus ACL scope in the key."},{"value":"question","label":"Just the question text, for 30 days."},{"value":"none","label":"Never cache. Cost is someone else's problem."}]}],"failureTitle":"A cached answer cites a fee schedule that changed yesterday","failureBody":"The policy was reindexed. The cache still holds yesterday's prose, with yesterday's citation. Separately, a desk pasted the assistant into a loop and the large-model bill crossed the control limit. Write the cache key and the budget behavior.","reviewIntro":"Eval is the SLO. The cache key is a consistency decision. The token budget is capacity.","review":["Ship only when the eval clears. Route small, then large, and stop escalating when the desk budget is exhausted: return a refusal or a queued review, do not silently overrun. Cache key includes versions. On policy publish, old keys miss.","Online thumbs-down is a stream you sample back into the eval set. It does not replace the set. JPMorgan Chase wants the superseded-policy case in the set permanently. Google wants the cost number in the design, not in the finance review after launch."],"rubric":[["Scope","Gate, route, cache. The retriever already exists."],["Estimates","A token budget and an eval size"],["Source of truth","Versioned documents. Cached answers derived."],["Diagram","Sync route with a budget. Async eval refresh."],["Deep dive","The cache key"],["Failure","Old answers miss after publish. Over-budget stops escalating."],["Tradeoff","More refusals and smaller models, under a cost cap"]]},{"file":"week-13-financial-rag","weekLabel":"Week 13 · Documents","title":"RAG over financial documents","minutes":"90 min","company":"JPMorgan Chase / Capital One","timed":false,"brief":"Design an assistant that answers from policy, filings, and client documents. The failure that matters is showing one client's KYC to another desk, or quoting a policy that was superseded yesterday.","goal":"","idea":"Embeddings are a derived index. The source documents are the truth, with versions and entitlements. Filter entitlement before the model sees chunks. If retrieval is empty, the model refuses. Deletes in the source must delete the index.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 2 and 12 — Document model and a rebuildable index","ddiaBody":"Chapter 2: documents are the right model for policies, and access control is part of the data model, not a prompt. Chapter 12: the vector index is derived. If you cannot rebuild it, you cannot honor a deletion.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: JPMorgan Chase and Capital One","barBody":"JPMorgan Chase grades tenant isolation and retention. Capital One grades citations and what happens when the policy version changes. A Google interviewer would also want the freshness and the empty-retrieval behavior. QPS is the easy part.","caseBody":"Netflix retrieves candidate titles and then ranks a small set. Steal that shape: retrieve a small entitled set, then let the model answer only from those chunks. They do not retrieve the whole catalog into the request. You do not retrieve the whole document lake into the prompt.","caseUrl":"https://netflixtechblog.com/system-architectures-for-personalization-and-recommendation-e081aa94b5d8","caseLink":"Netflix Tech Blog — candidates, then a small ranked set","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"Before","label":"Entitlement filter, relative to the model"},{"value":"Version","label":"What a citation must include"},{"value":"Empty","label":"Retrieval that must refuse"},{"value":"Delete","label":"Source delete must hit the index"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Source","Document plus version plus ACL","Truth"],["Index","Chunks and embeddings, derived","Rebuild and delete"],["Query","ACL predicate first, then similarity","Isolation"],["Answer","Cites chunk ids or refuses","Audit"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"acl","prompt":"When is entitlement applied?","sound":"before","staff":"Before retrieval returns chunks to the model. A filter after generation still put another client's KYC in the prompt, which is the leak. JPMorgan Chase will stop you there. The index query carries the caller's desks and the document ACL.","options":[{"value":"before","label":"ACL in the query. The model never sees other tenants."},{"value":"after","label":"Retrieve broadly, drop bad chunks after the model answers."},{"value":"prompt","label":"Tell the model not to mention other clients."}]},{"id":"fresh","prompt":"A policy is superseded. What is retrieved?","sound":"versioned","staff":"The current version, with the version id in the citation. Incremental indexing for policy changes, not a weekly full rebuild as the only path. Yesterday's fee schedule is a regulatory defect even if the embedding is pretty.","options":[{"value":"versioned","label":"Incremental index. Citation includes version."},{"value":"weekly","label":"Weekly rebuild only. Close enough."},{"value":"forever","label":"Keep every version in the prompt and let the model choose."}]},{"id":"cite","prompt":"What must an answer contain?","sound":"citations","staff":"Citations to chunk ids the user is entitled to, or a refusal if retrieval is empty or below a threshold you set. A fluent answer with no source is not an assistant a bank can ship. Capital One wants the refusal path tested.","options":[{"value":"citations","label":"Cite entitled chunks, or refuse."},{"value":"fluent","label":"Answer fluently. Citations are a later project."},{"value":"dump","label":"Paste the top 50 documents into the prompt every time."}]}],"failureTitle":"Another client's KYC is in the context window","failureBody":"A shared index has no tenant predicate. The model answers a question about fees using another client's onboarding pack, and also quotes a policy PDF that was replaced yesterday. Write the query predicate, the citation, and the delete path.","reviewIntro":"ACL first, version in the citation, index derived and deletable.","review":["Sync path: authenticate, query vectors with the ACL predicate, require citations, refuse otherwise, log the chunk ids. Async path: chunk and embed on document write, tombstone embeddings on delete, reindex a superseded policy.","The log is the audit JPMorgan Chase will ask for: who asked, which chunks were retrieved, which version was cited. Embeddings of a deleted KYC document are still that document. Chapter 12 means the derived index honors the delete."],"rubric":[["Scope","Entitled Q&A, not autonomous trading"],["Estimates","Index lag for a policy change, not hero QPS"],["Source of truth","Versioned documents. Embeddings derived."],["Diagram","Sync retrieve-then-answer. Async index."],["Deep dive","Tenant predicate"],["Failure","Empty or forbidden retrieval refuses. Delete hits the index."],["Tradeoff","More refusals, so a cross-tenant answer cannot ship"]]},{"file":"week-14-mock-interview","weekLabel":"Week 14 · Mock","title":"Closed-book mock","minutes":"45 min drill","company":"Google panel, financial firm rubric","timed":true,"brief":"Closed book. Forty-five minutes. No case debrief until you commit. Write the journal as if a Google-trained interviewer is in the room and the rubric is Revolut, Visa, Capital One, and JPMorgan Chase.","goal":"","idea":"The seven moves are the structure. The financial weighting is the content of the deep dive. You do not need a new trick. You need to finish the page.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA, the map you already have","ddiaBody":"Use chapter 7 for the posting, chapter 8 for the timeout, chapter 11 for the webhook stream, chapter 12 for the score and the feed. Name the chapter only if it helps you state the tradeoff. Do not recite the table of contents.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: Microsoft and Google structure, financial-firm invariants","barBody":"Structure: scope, arithmetic, API and data, sync plus async, one deep dive, failure, tradeoff. Content: ledger, idempotency, reconciliation, audit. The review opens only after a real journal is in the box.","caseBody":"Debrief, after you commit. A hire-level mock names the ledger as source of truth in the first third, shows storage arithmetic from 150/s not from 1,500/s, draws a sync decision plus an async webhook, and deep-dives either the duplicate webhook or the model timeout. The other one is still mentioned. Missing a component is fine. Missing the idempotency key is not. Staff judgment is still earned by shipping, not by this page.","caseUrl":"https://www.uber.com/blog/michelangelo-machine-learning-platform/","caseLink":"After the mock: Michelangelo, if you want the case again","codeLab":"","sealed":"Prompt. Design the payment-risk slice: card attempt, fraud score with a deadline, ledger, processor webhook that can duplicate, human review for a small tail. 8 million customers. 150 attempts per second average, 1,500 peak. Model budget 40 ms inside a 200 ms decision. About 2 percent go to review. A duplicate webhook must not move money twice. The model being down must have a named outcome. You have 45 minutes. Fill the journal with the seven moves. Estimates use peak for latency and average for storage.","numbers":[{"value":"8M","label":"Customers"},{"value":"150 /s","label":"Average attempts"},{"value":"1,500 /s","label":"Peak attempts"},{"value":"40 ms","label":"Model budget"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Storage math","Start from 150/s, not the peak","Disk"],["Latency math","Start from 1,500/s and 200 ms","Fleet"],["Review load","2 percent of attempts","Analyst queue"],["Keys","Attempt, processor event, review action","Three, if you have time"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"deep","prompt":"Which deep dive will you actually finish?","sound":"webhook-or-model","staff":"Pick one and finish it: duplicate webhook, or model timeout. Mention the other in a sentence. A tour of both at half depth is the usual miss at Google and Microsoft. Financial interviewers still need the key and the ledger invariant inside the one you picked.","options":[{"value":"webhook-or-model","label":"One of those two, finished, with the invariant."},{"value":"tour","label":"A short comment on every box."},{"value":"cdn","label":"The CDN and the homepage, to show breadth."}]},{"id":"math","prompt":"Which rate sizes disk?","sound":"average","staff":"Average, 150/s, times payload, times retention, times replication. Peak sizes the fleet and the p99. Using peak for disk overbuilds by 10x and still skips the latency story. Say the arithmetic in the journal, even roughly.","options":[{"value":"average","label":"Average for disk. Peak for fleet and p99."},{"value":"peak","label":"Peak for disk and fleet."},{"value":"skip","label":"Skip numbers. The diagram is enough."}]},{"id":"cut","prompt":"What will you explicitly leave out?","sound":"named","staff":"Name a cut: disputes, multi-region, or the model-training pipeline. Google and Microsoft grade the cut. A financial interviewer is fine with it if the ledger, the key, and the webhook are in. Leaving out idempotency is not a cut. It is a miss.","options":[{"value":"named","label":"A named cut that is not the invariant."},{"value":"nothing","label":"Cover every subsystem briefly."},{"value":"ledger","label":"Leave the ledger for later. Start with the model."}]}],"failureTitle":"The prompt's failure is the deep dive","failureBody":"Choose one: the processor delivers the same webhook twice, or the model misses 40 ms and the merchant retries. Write what is durable before the ack, what a retry returns, and what must not be posted twice. Put that in the failure note and in the journal.","reviewIntro":"Score the journal. 10 of 14, with failure and idempotency at 2, is the hire-level bar for this mock. Staff judgment is the next job, not this page.","review":["A strong journal opens with users and a cut, does the arithmetic, names postings as source of truth, draws sync decision and async webhook, deep-dives one failure with a key, and says the tradeoff: fallback latency over a late score, or pending-and-reconcile over a guessed status.","If the journal never says what happens when the webhook is a duplicate, run this drill again tomorrow. Do not start a new topic. The companies you are practicing for all notice that gap, including Google and Microsoft, who will call it a failed retry story."],"rubric":[["Scope","Named cut, risk slice still intact"],["Estimates","Average for disk, peak for p99, arithmetic visible"],["Source of truth","Postings, said early"],["Diagram","Sync decision, async webhook and review"],["Deep dive","One of the two failures, finished"],["Failure","A key, a replay, no second posting"],["Tradeoff","One sentence a skeptical interviewer can repeat"]]},{"file":"week-14-payment-risk-capstone","weekLabel":"Week 14 · Capstone","title":"Payment-risk platform","minutes":"90 min","company":"Revolut / Capital One / Visa / JPMorgan Chase","timed":false,"brief":"Capstone. A model scores a payment. Humans review a tail. The ledger is the source of truth. A duplicate processor webhook cannot move money twice. This is the system the earlier weeks were building.","goal":"","idea":"Assemble the pieces. Do not invent a new database. Decision row, ledger posting, outbox, webhook idempotency, model deadline, analyst queue, reconciliation. One deep dive is enough if it is the duplicate webhook plus the model timeout together.","ddiaTitle":"DDIA chapters 7, 8, 11, and 12 — The whole book on one path","ddiaBody":"Chapter 7 commits money once. Chapter 8 makes the webhook and the model timeout unknown rather than false. Chapter 11 carries the outbox and the review queue. Chapter 12 keeps the score and the activity feed derived from the ledger and the decision.","barTitle":"Who is grading this hour: the financial firms, told in Google's seven moves","barBody":"Revolut and Monzo hear the invariants. Visa hears auth versus webhook. Capital One hears model-down. JPMorgan Chase hears audit and no silent overwrite. Google and Microsoft still want scope, arithmetic, one deep dive, and a tradeoff. Hit the seven moves. Then hit the invariant.","caseBody":"Michelangelo for the score as a versioned, derived signal. The Simian Army stance for the duplicate webhook and the dead GPU. Open Connect is not this system. If you reach for a CDN, you are in the wrong deep dive.","caseUrl":"https://www.uber.com/blog/michelangelo-machine-learning-platform/","caseLink":"Uber Engineering — Michelangelo, the score is not the ledger","codeLab":"","sealed":"","numbers":[{"value":"1,500 /s","label":"Peak payment attempts"},{"value":"200 ms","label":"Decision p99"},{"value":"40 ms","label":"Model budget"},{"value":"2%","label":"Human review tail"}],"tableHeaders":["Given","Value","What it sizes"],"tableRows":[["Attempt key","Client idempotency key","One charge"],["Webhook key","Processor event id","One transition"],["Model","Deadline then fallback","Decision row records which"],["Review","Async, timeout, same state machine","Append-only"]],"words":[],"choices":[{"id":"truth","prompt":"What is the source of truth for money?","sound":"ledger","staff":"The ledger of balanced postings. The model score, the webhook body, the analyst note, and the activity feed are derived or are inputs. They do not overwrite a posting. This is the sentence the capstone exists to force.","options":[{"value":"ledger","label":"Postings. Everything else is an input or a projection."},{"value":"score","label":"The model score. The ledger follows it."},{"value":"webhook","label":"The processor webhook."}]},{"id":"together","prompt":"Model timeout and a duplicate webhook happen on the same payment. What wins?","sound":"keys","staff":"The attempt key makes one decision, fallback included. The processor event id makes one state transition. Order them with the state machine. A second webhook does not post. A model that wakes up later does not create a second decision for that attempt. It may inform an analyst, asynchronously.","options":[{"value":"keys","label":"One decision per attempt. One transition per processor event."},{"value":"latest","label":"Whichever signal arrives last overwrites the payment."},{"value":"both","label":"Apply the fallback and every webhook as separate postings."}]},{"id":"human","prompt":"What may a human reviewer change?","sound":"transition","staff":"A legal transition on the state machine, audited, idempotent, with a reason. They may not edit a posting amount in place. JPMorgan Chase wants the reason on the trail. Revolut wants the customer-visible state to match.","options":[{"value":"transition","label":"A legal, audited transition. Append-only."},{"value":"edit","label":"Edit the posting to the amount they think is fair."},{"value":"model","label":"Retrain the model on the spot and auto-apply it to open payments."}]}],"failureTitle":"Duplicate webhook and a model timeout on one payment","failureBody":"The model misses its deadline. You record a fallback decision and the response to the merchant is lost, so they retry the same attempt. Meanwhile the processor delivers the same webhook twice. An analyst has the payment in the tail queue. Write the rows that exist, and the rows that must not.","reviewIntro":"If this review feels like a repeat, the program worked. Check your journal for a missing key or a silent overwrite.","review":["Rows that exist: one payment, one decision for that attempt key noting fallback, one posting or hold, outbox events, one webhook apply for that processor event, one analyst task. Rows that must not: a second posting for the retry, a second posting for the duplicate webhook, an edited amount.","Tradeoff to say out loud: the fallback is less accurate than a late model, and you accept that so the terminal gets one answer. Analysts add accuracy after, on a timer, without a second charge. Peak 1,500/s is not the hard part. The keys are."],"rubric":[["Scope","Score, ledger, webhook, review. Cut anything else."],["Estimates","1,500/s peak, 200 ms, 40 ms"],["Source of truth","Postings"],["Diagram","Sync decision and post. Async webhook, review, feed."],["Deep dive","The double failure in the prompt"],["Failure","Attempt key and processor event id"],["Tradeoff","A faster fallback over a late score"]]}];
